The normalized two-field scalar potential, $\tilde{V} = V/M_X^4$, is shown as a function of the dimensionless variables $x = \phi/M$ and $y = \chi/M$, evaluated along the slice $s = 0$. We assume the critical value $\phi_c = M$, and set the parameter $g = \lambda M / (2 M_X) = 0.1$. The green point marks the onset of observable inflation along the inflationary valley ($\chi = 0$), and the red point indicates the critical (or "waterfall") point, where inflation ends due to the tachyonic instability in the $\chi$ direction. The blue point denotes the global minimum of the potential, where the fields settle after inflation ends and spontaneous symmetry breaking occurs.
