\section{Introduction}
% Context and Domain Importance
 %\IEEEPARstart{R}{eliability} assessment is essential for evaluating measurement instruments, such as questionnaires, surveys, or other tools used across various domains, including psychology \cite{samra2011design}, healthcare \cite{lu2024wearable, xue2024instrumentation}, education \cite{zheng2018emotionmeter, martin2021future}, and engineering \cite{cruz2020testing}. It ensures that items within an instrument are internally consistent and collectively measure the intended construct or concept. Internal consistency refers to the extent to which all items in a measurement instrument contribute to measuring the same latent construct, providing a cohesive and unified evaluation. Cronbach's Alpha is a widely used measure of internal consistency and has long been considered a standard.  However, despite its popularity, Cronbach's Alpha relies on correlative assumptions that often fail in complex, real-world datasets, resulting in misleading conclusions \cite{tavakol2011making, karakaya2022sample}. While alternative measures like McDonald's Omega provide greater flexibility by accounting for varying item contributions \cite{hayes2020use}, they remain sensitive to factor model assumptions, redundant items, and normality requirements. This stresses the need for new techniques to address these challenges without reliance on restrictive assumptions.

% Context and Domain Importance
\IEEEPARstart{R}{eliability} assessment is essential for evaluating survey-based instruments used in human-centered domains such as human-robot collaboration \cite{hoffman2019evaluating}, healthcare \cite{lu2024wearable}, AI-generated content evaluation \cite{10869828} and education \cite{martin2021future}. The assessment ensures that elements within survey questionnaires are internally consistent and collectively measure the intended construct, affirming the truthfulness of the collected data \cite{hayes2020use, ahmed2021reliability}. Internal consistency is a type of reliability assessment that measures how well items in a survey-based instrument contribute to evaluating the same latent construct. Thus, internal consistency is essential for assessing any measuring methodology for collecting primary data. It also enhances the validity of research findings and strengthens their credibility, making it essential for rigorous scientific inquiry \cite{tavakol2011making, karakaya2022sample}. 

