In this letter, we develop a novel protocol for the efficient implementation of a single-qutrit gate set by coherent manipulating SU(3) dynamics.
Analogous to their qubit counterparts, we introduce qutrit Hadamard (H) and X gates serving as primitive operations in qutrit-based quantum computation \cite{wang2020qudits}.
Both gates are demonstrated on a transmon \cite{Koch2007}, a fabrication-friendly superconducting circuit \cite{you2011,gu2017,krantz2019,Blais2021}, achieving an average fidelity of 99.5\% with a gate time of 35 ns.
To explore the capability of the protocol, we implement two key quantum circuits. The first is qutrit Ramsey interferometry \cite{shlyakhov2018quantum,suppmaterials} analogous to a triple-slit experiment, which hold potential for high-precision phase estimation and intrinsically outperform their qubit counterparts. The second is the parity-check algorithm \cite{gedik2015computational,suppmaterials}, achieving a special quantum speedup without requiring entanglement resources.
We further validate that the circuits are readily generalizing to higher-dimensional qudits.


\textit{\textcolor{blue}{Theoretical Framework}}---%
% We aim to efficiently construct the single-qutrit Clifford group $\mathcal{C}_3$ by analogy with standard qubit gate sets, incorporating the qutrit Pauli operations. 
% Conventional decomposition of arbitrary SU(3) gates into SU(2) operations, as illustrated in Fig. \ref{fig:1}(a), typically suffers from error accumulation and prolonged gate times.
% Augmenting $\mathcal{C}_3$ group with a non-Clifford gate, such as the T gate \cite{howard2012qudit}, facilitates universal single-qutrit control, enabling arbitrary unitaries to be approximated to any desired precision \cite{prakash2018normal}.
Unlike conventional decomposition of arbitrary SU(3) gates into SU(2) operations, as shown in Fig. \ref{fig:1}(a), we propose an efficient construction of the single-qutrit Clifford group $\mathcal{C}_3$ by analogy with standard qubit gate sets \cite{noteforC3}.
We find that a minimal generating set \{H, S\}, analogous to the qubit U3 decomposition \cite{mckay2017efficient}, could reduce coherent errors due to the homogeneity of calibration parameters, but at the cost of increased circuit depth (see Supplemental Material \cite{suppmaterials}). 
We instead employ the more efficient generating set \{H, S, X, Z\} \cite{liu2023performing}, noting that phase gates \{S, Z\} are implemented by using extended virtual phase control $\hat{P}$, a technique that is widely used in qubit systems, thereby eliminating the need for pulse sequences and simplifying hardware requirements \cite{mckay2017efficient}.
Hence, our efforts focus on the efficient implementation of H and X gates, which are respectively defined as \cite{ringbauer2022universal}
\begin{equation} \label{eq1}
\mathrm{H} = \frac{1}{\sqrt{3}} 
\begin{pmatrix}
 1  & 1 & 1 \\
 1  & \omega & \omega^{2}  \\
 1 & \omega^{2}  & \omega
\end{pmatrix},  \quad
\mathrm{X} = \begin{pmatrix}
 0  & 0 & 1 \\
  1  & 0 & 0 \\
 0 & 1 & 0
\end{pmatrix},
\end{equation}
where $\omega = \exp(i2\pi/3)$ is the primitive cube root of unity.


