We begin by characterizing the qutrit gates through an investigation of population dynamics.
Fig.~\ref{fig:2}(a) presents the results for the H gate, where the transmon is initialized in states $\ket{0}$, $\ket{1}$, and $\ket{2}$, shown from top to bottom, respectively. Each initialization is followed by a 35 ns control pulse tailored for the H gate.
The Hamiltonian Eq. \eqref{eq2} exhibits a parity-like symmetry $PH(t)P=H(t)$, which arises from the condition $\Omega_1(t) = \Omega_2(t)$ with $P = \ket{0}\bra{2} + \ket{2}\bra{0}$, resulting in mirrored dynamics.
Similarly, for the X gate, as shown in Fig.~\ref{fig:2}(b), the distinct chiral properties revealed by population transfer are evident.
Importantly, time-reversal of the control pulses implements the inverse operation $\mathrm{X}^{-1}$.
In this case, the Hamiltonian Eq. \eqref{eq2} possesses a symmetry $PH(t)P=H(T-t)$, assuming $\Omega_1(t) = \Omega_2(T-t)$ and $\Delta = 0$,  thereby yielding $\mathcal{P}\mathcal{T}$-symmetry dynamics.
% The close agreement between experimental measurements and numerical simulations, in turn, confirms the validity for both gates.
The results indicate the high-fidelity implementation of both H and X gates, demonstrating efficient and coherent control over the qutrit dynamics.
