Next, we construct the Clifford gate set $\mathcal{C}_3$, as established for the qubit, consisting of 216 distinct elements, generated by the set \{X, Z, H, S\}.
Given that Z and S gates are executed as virtual phase gates, the average number of gates per Clifford operation is 1.75 H, 1.51 S, 0.54 X, and 0.52 Z gates  \cite{suppmaterials}.
Routinely, to assess the quality of the qutrit gates, we employ randomized benchmarking (RB), a widely used technique that estimates the average gate error while mitigating the influence of state preparation and measurement errors \cite{morvan2021qutrit, kononenko2021characterization}.
As shown in Fig. \ref{fig:3}(a), we perform the standard qutrit RB by repeatedly applying gate sequences that are randomly sampled from the single-qutrit Clifford group $\mathcal{C}_3$ to the transmon qutrit. 
Due to the inherent properties of the Clifford group, averaging over these randomly sampled sequences will lead to a depolarizing channel.
The resulting exponential decay of the population allows us to extract the depolarization parameter $p_{c}$.
From this, the average error per Clifford gate is $r_{c}=0.0102$, given by $r_{c} = (1-p_{c})(1-1/d)$, where $d=3$ represents the dimension of the qutrit Hilbert space and $p_c$ is the depolarization parameter extracted from exponential fit.


To characterize specific Clifford gates, especially the H and X gates of interest, we adopt interleaved RB (IRB), in which the target gate is interleaved after each Clifford gate in the standard RB sequence.
The average gate error can be determined by comparing the depolarization parameters obtained with and without the interleaved gate, then given by $r_{g}=(1-p_{g}/p_{c})(1-1/d)$, where $p_{g}$ is the depolarization parameter obtained from the IRB.
From the experimental results, we find the individual average gate errors for the H gate $r_{\mathrm{H}}=0.0045$ and for the X gate $r_{\mathrm{X}}=0.0046$.
The population of the state $\left | 0\right \rangle$ exhibits an exponential decay towards an asymptotic value of $1/3$, indicating that the qutrit indeed suffers a depolarizing channel.
Notebly, the extracted average errors are consistent with the decoherence-induced error limit, suggesting that coherent errors have been effectively suppressed through our calibration procedure \cite{suppmaterials}.
The corresponding average gate fidelity are then determined as $F_{g} = 1 - r_{g}$, 
yielding fidelities of approximately 99.5\% for both H and X gates.
%
Moreover, to further characterize the long-time stability of our experimental setup, we monitor the average gate error by repeatedly performing IRB over a 18-hour period without any recalibration.
As shown in Fig.~\ref{fig:3}(b), the gate errors remain stable for each gate throughout the monitoring duration, confirming that our protocol can be reliably executed over prolonged periods on current devices.

