At last, we demonstrate the applications of our protocol by executing two paradigmatic quantum circuits, which can be extend to single-qudit scenarios.
All involved qutrit gates are directly implemented in one step via coherent control, thereby showcasing the advantage of our protocol in achieving efficient implementation.
The first, as shown in Fig.~\ref{fig:4}(a), is qutrit Ramsey interferometry, an approach widely used in quantum metrology \cite{shlyakhov2018quantum,suppmaterials}.
Theoretically, the final populations $P_{j}$ of the qutrit quantum states $\ket{j}$, ($j=0, 1, 2$) depend on the delayed phase $\phi$, and can be expressed as
\begin{equation}
P_{j}=\frac{1}{9} [1 + 2\cos(\phi - 2\pi j/3)]^{2}.
\end{equation}
The experimental results exhibit overall agreement with theoretical predictions.
Combined with the Kitaev algorithm \cite{danilin2018quantum,chi2022programmable}, the qutrit Ramsey sequence enables high-precision phase extraction.
In particular, 
we further propose the probability density of obtaining a phase estimate $\tilde{\phi}$ after $N$ iterations given by
\begin{align}
p(\tilde{\phi}|\phi ) &\propto \frac{1}{2\pi d^{N}}  \frac{\sin^{2}(d^{N}  \delta \phi /2)}{\sin^{2}( \delta \phi /2)}.
\end{align}
As the iterations $N$ or the dimension $d$ increases, the distribution becomes progressively sharper, with the width of the main peak scaling as $1/d^{N}$.
Consequently, the qudit Ramsey interferometry allows to extract more information per iteration, reducing the number of individual measurements required to achieve a given degree of precision by a factor of $O(\log{d})$ compared to the qubit case.


The other is the well-known parity-check algorithm designed to determine whether a given element permutation is even or odd.
Generally, a qudit initialized in the state $\ket{m}$ is transformed into the Fourier basis $\ket{f_{m}} = \frac{1}{\sqrt{d}}{\textstyle \sum_{k=0}^{d-1}}  \chi_{m}(k) \ket{k}$ via the qudit $\mathrm{H}$ gate, where $\chi_{m}(k) = \omega_d^{mk}$ with $\omega_d = \exp(i2\pi/d)$ denotes an irreducible character of the abelian group $\mathbb{Z}_{d}$ when $\gcd(m,d)=1$.
After applying the permutation operator $U_{\pi}$, the inverse Fourier transform returns the state to the computational basis.
The resulting final state can be expressed as
$
\ket{\psi_{f}}= \frac{1}{d}\sum_{n=0}^{d-1} \sum_{k=0}^{d-1} \chi_{m}(k)  \chi_{n}^{\ast } (\pi(k))  \ket{n}.
$
When $\pi$ is an even permutation, it can be represented as a cyclic shift by $r$, i.e., $\pi(k) = k + r \mod d$. The character then transforms as $\chi_n(\pi(k)) = \omega^{n(k + r)} = \omega^{nr} \chi_n(k)$. For an odd permutation of the form $\pi(k) = -k + r \mod d$, the character becomes $\chi_n(\pi(k)) = \omega^{n(-k + r)} = \omega^{nr}\chi_{-n}(k)$. Utilizing the orthogonality of the irreducible characters of $\mathbb{Z}_{d}$, the final state satisfies
\begin{equation}
\ket{\psi_{f}} = 
\begin{cases} 
\ket{m} & \text{for even permutation,}  \\ 
\ket{d-m} & \text{for odd permutation.} 
\end{cases}
\end{equation}
For suitable choices of the initial state $\ket{m}$, with $\gcd(m,d)=1$, even and odd permutations yield orthogonal final states, enabling deterministic parity discrimination.
As a result, shown in Fig.~\ref{fig:4}(b),
a qutrit initialized in the state $\ket{1}$ will deterministically evolve into the state $\ket{1}$ for even permutations and into the state $\ket{2}$ for odd permutations, with no population expected in the state $\ket{0}$.
Notably, a classical algorithm requires at least two queries to determine the parity of a permutation, that is one to identify the cyclic shift and another to determine its direction. In contrast, the quantum algorithm enables parity determination with a single query, achieving a two-to-one speedup without requiring entanglement \cite{gedik2015computational}.


\textit{\textcolor{blue}{Conclusions}}---%
In summary, we present a simple, efficient one-step protocol for implementing a qutrit gate set by directly manipulating the three-dimensional Hilbert space.
This approach analytically yields the required control pulses, circumventing the complexity of SU(3) Lie algebra and eliminating the need for gate decomposition or optimal control.
As a proof-of-principle demonstration, we experimentally design H and X gates within 35 ns, achieving a reliable fidelity of 99.5\%.
Furthermore, we examine the advantages of our protocol by applying it to two application-relevant quantum circuits, validating its feasibility for qutrit computation.
Notably, the underlying idea of the protocol is platform-independent, suggesting its applicability across various quantum systems.
% Application to relevant quantum circuits confirms our protocol viability for qutrit computation.
% Notably, the underlying idea of the protocol is platform-independent, enabling broad applicability.
% By efficiently implementing a single-qutrit gate set, we complete universal qutrit computation for prevalent platforms.
Our work lays the foundation for further exploration into the systematic design of qudit gates.




