\subsection*{Appendix}
The example response provided in the main text of Ref.~\cite{barrie2025emergent} suggests that the game takes place between two players. Further inspection of \cite{barrie2025emergent}'s data repository confirms that this is not unique: across different models, the majority of responses similarly used the term ``both'', or otherwise implied that the prompt described a two-player game.
Following the meta-prompting approach conducted by Barrie \& T\"ornberg \cite{barrie2025emergent}, we also tested whether the LLM infers that it is engaged in a social experiment with a population, or instead interprets the experiment as involving only two players. We used the same prompt template as in \cite{barrie2025emergent}, replacing the final question about global convergence with the following: \textit{``Finally, do you think that you are playing against a single player or a whole population?''}
This meta-prompting test was repeated five times for each tested model\footnote{Namely, Claude-3-5-Sonnet-20240620, Claude-3-5-Haiku-20241022, Claude-3-Opus-20240229, GPT-4.1, GPT-4o, GPT-4o-Mini, Llama-3-70B Instruct, and Llama-3.1-70B Instruct.}. Every response across all tested models indicated that the experiment involves two players, rather than a population. However, the most capable LLMs also correctly noted that if the experiment were conducted in populations, both the optimal strategy and the overall convergence dynamics would depend on the evolution of the system. They did not, however, interpret this as the setup described in the prompt (see GPT-4.1 example reported below). In this regard, it is important to stress that in the experiments of \cite{ashery2025emergent}, and in the naming game more broadly, agents are never presented with information about ``the frequency of choices in the population'' (as mentioned in the GPT-4.1 example) or any other metrics describing collective behaviour.

%For example, one response from gpt-4.1 states that the \textit{``The description is framed as a two-player game, so you are playing against a single other player. If you were playing against a population, the payoff might be based on the frequency of choices in the population (e.g., evolutionary game theory), but here, your outcome depends only on the simultaneous choice of one other agent''}. This response correctly identifies a potential scenario that is similar to our experimental setup, but does not interpret it as the setup described in the prompt. This illustrates that while the model is capable of reasoning about population-level dynamics, it does not apply such reasoning to the given prompt. 

%The models we tested are Claude-3-5-Sonnet-20240620, Claude-3-5-Haiku-20241022, Claude-3-Opus-20240229, GPT-4.1, GPT-4o, GPT-4o-Mini, Llama-3-70B Instruct, and Llama-3.1-70B Instruct. They all reported ``single player'' in all five repetitions of the population question.

