\subsection{Throughput fairness}
\label{result_fairness}
We use Jain's fairness index\cite{Jain} to evaluate the throughput fairness between the networks. The Jain index measures quantitative fairness. The value of Jain's fairness index is between 0 and 1. Fairer network throughput enhances channel access fairness as the index approaches one. Jain's throughput fairness index is calculated using the following equation:% $J=\frac{[\,\sum_{i=1}^n{x_i}]\,^2}{n\sum_{i=1}^n {x^2_i}}$.
\begin{equation} \label{eqn1}
%\footnotesize
	J=\frac{[\,\sum_{i=1}^n{x_i}]\,^2}{n\sum_{i=1}^n {x^2_i}}
\end{equation}
where $x_i$ is the mean throughput of $i^{th}$ network and $n$ is the number of networks. 
