\section{Introduction}
% \textcolor{blue}{
Deep learning-based AI models, often referred to as ``Software 2.0''~\cite{ZhuW022,ZhangLLCLGC23}, have become a prominent research focus in the software engineering community. 
Federated Learning (FL) is an emerging distributed AI deployment paradigm that enables multiple participants to collaboratively train models without sharing their private data~\cite{YangLCT19,abs-2304-01829,McMahanMRHA17}.
A specific variant, Vertical FL (VFL), is particularly well-suited for cross-silo settings, where different participants (e.g., companies) hold complementary feature data for the same set of users.
VFL adopts a pay-as-you-use crowdsourcing software strategy during inference.
In this setting, the task party (\task) initiates a crowdsourced inference task, aiming to leverage the data held by the data party (\data) to perform joint inference while preserving privacy.
\task submits an inference request to \data and compensates \data based on the volume of inference performed.
Then \data loads the target data and trained model into its local inference software, executes the computation, and returns the results to \task.
% }
% \myparagraph{Vertical Federated Learning (VFL).}
% Federated Learning (FL) enables multiple participants to jointly train a machine learning model without sharing private data~\cite{YangLCT19,abs-2304-01829,McMahanMRHA17}.
% Vertical FL (VFL) is a type of FL in which two participants hold \textit{different features} for the \textit{same users}.
% The participant holding the labels is the \textit{task party} \task and the other is the data party \data~\cite{YangLCT19}. 
% \textcolor{blue}{
% VFL also includes a third-party coordinator~\cite{YangLCT19,abs-2304-01829} to coordinate VFL processes, such as encrypting intermediate parameters, maintaining logs and audit trails to ensure regulatory compliance, etc.
% }

% \myparagraph{Target Scenario.}
% VFL is widely used in scenarios such as e-commerce, healthcare, and
% Fintech~\cite{LiuKZPHYOZY24,abs-2405-02364,HuangW023}.
% \textcolor{blue}{
% In the FinTech sector, institutions often need to assess the credit risk of users applying for loans, credit cards, or buy-now-pay-later services.
% However, no single party has all the information needed for robust credit evaluation due to privacy, regulation, and data silos.
% A digital bank (\task) that holds loan application data and historical repayment behavior for a group of users.
% A mobile payment (\data) platform or e-wallet provider (e.g., Alipay, PayPal) that has rich user transaction histories, spending patterns, and behavioral data for the same users.
% $\mathcal{P}_t$ aims to collaborate with $\mathcal{P}_d$ through VFL to predict user's credit score without leaking each participant' data to other participants.
% }
% The business model of VFL is that \task asks \data to train a model for free.
% During joint inference, \task requests \data to use its data for inference on the trained local model to assist \task's predictions and pay \data by the amount of data used.
% \textcolor{blue}{
% This paper focuses on the peak inference scenario--periods of high-throughput, low-latency prediction traffic such as shopping festivals (e.g., Singles Day, Cyber Monday) and month-end paydays.
% Ensuring fast, scalable joint inference under these peaks is critical to delivering real-time credit decisions and maintaining user satisfaction~\cite{SoiferLLZLHZOMB19,GujaratiKAHKVM20}.
% }

\myparagraph{Motivation.}
Accountability is a crucial concept for building responsible AI software~\cite{responsible-ai}.
Accountability refers to the relationship between an agent and a principal.
The principal delegates specific tasks to the agent, who acts according to the principal's requirements.
In the VFL inference phase, \data (agent) receives the inference request from \task (principal) and uses the target data and the trained model for inference.
The core of accountability is that the agent should demonstrate the legitimacy of its actions to the principal~\cite{novelli2024accountability,ai-accountability}.
However, \textit{existing VFL lacks a mechanism to audit the correctness of the execution of inference software on \data.}
Thus, \task cannot ascertain the legitimacy of the data or model used by \data, nor the integrity of the \data's inference process.
If \data employs incorrect models or data during inference, the resulting representations may be inaccurate or misleading.
When \task uses these unreliable representations for downstream predictions, it risks making erroneous decisions.
This issue is particularly critical in high-throughput, low-latency inference scenarios, where incorrect execution can lead to substantial economic consequences.
A notable example is the Knight Capital Group incident, where a versioning error in trading software led to a \$440 million loss~\cite{knight-capital}.
These risks highlight the importance of auditing the inference process to ensure correctness--an aspect that is currently overlooked in VFL research.
To the best of our knowledge, this is the first work to systematically address this problem.
% Transamerica units were fined \$97.6 million for failing to inform investors that certain investment models were flawed and did not work as expected~\cite{Transamerica}.
% Knight Capital Group lost \$440 million due to a version error in the software in its trading system~\cite{knight-capital}.
% These highlight how the absence of auditing mechanisms--and the resulting lack of accountability--can lead to severe consequences.
% \textit{To the best of our knowledge, this is the first work to discuss the problem of a lack of inference auditing and its security consequences in VFL.}

\myparagraph{Insight.}
The core challenge is resolving the privacy-efficiency-auditing tradeoff: \task must audit the correctness of \data's inference software execution during large-scale inference without accessing \data's private data or introducing online latency.
% The challenge lies in resolving the privacy-efficiency-auditing contradiction, where \task needs to audit the execution correctness of \data's inference software during large-scale inference, without accessing \data's privacy data or introducing additional online inference latency.
To address this, we propose a \underline{Ve}rtical \underline{F}ederated \underline{I}nference \underline{A}udit framework, \textbf{\method} built on Trusted Execution Environments (TEE; e.g., Intel SGX~\footnote{We mainly use SGX because it is a mature product available on many commercial platforms and machines~\cite{sgx_lenovo_thinksystem,sgx_available_blog}.}).
% \textcolor{blue}{
\method minimizes the trusted computing base by relying on TEE enclaves and introduces dual execution paths: one via \data's untrusted inference software, and another via a trusted path using TEE and a semi-honest coordinator (TEE-COO).
To balance privacy and performance, \method partitions \data's model--executing privacy--sensitive shallow layers inside \data's TEE, and deeper layers on coordinator \coord.
Inspired by N-version programming~\cite{chen1978n,avizienis1995methodology}, where multiple software versions run in parallel to detect errors, \method allows \task to compare the results from these two paths.
Any mismatch between trusted and untrusted results enables fault detection and attribution, identifying errors or tampering in \data's inference process.
This redundancy empowers \task with a robust, low-latency auditing mechanism that ensures trustworthy execution without compromising privacy.
% The use of a TEE facilitates minimizing the trusted computing base, as trust is placed in the secure enclave's operations.
% \method creates a form of redundancy by having two computation paths for the \data's inference: an untrusted inference software execution path and a trusted path utilizing a TEE and a semi-honest coordinator (TEE-COO).
% \method's trusted path balances privacy and computational load by splitting the \data's model, running sensitive shallow layers in the \data's TEE and less sensitive deep layers in the coordinator \coord.
% Similar to N-version programming, where multiple software versions run in parallel to detect errors, \method allows \task to compare the results from these two paths.
% Consequently, any inconsistencies between the trusted and untrusted results lead to fault detection and isolation, attributing potential errors or malicious tampering to the \data's inference process.
% \method establishes a robust auditing capability, crucial for accountability, as \task can verify that \data is executed inference software correctly.
% }

\myparagraph{Technical Challenges.}
However, there are three technical challenges in designing an efficient auditing framework based on TEE.
%
\textbf{C1: Privacy Leak Control.}
The first challenge is to control the information flow when outsourcing parts of the \data's local model to \coord for collaborative inference, thereby mitigating the risk of \data's input privacy leakage.
Delegating model computation to \coord creates an interface, and \coord may infer \data's private data from the intermediate data transmitted by the interface~\cite{HeZL19,YinZZLYCH23,PasquiniAB21}.
% The first challenge is how to mitigate the privacy leakage of inference outsourcing.
% Previous research has shown that outsourcing part of the local model to the \coord for inference can leak the \data's input privacy~\cite{HeZL19,YinZZLYCH23,PasquiniAB21}.
% Given that the primary objective of VFL is to preserve participant privacy, it is crucial to ensure that the input privacy of the \data remains protected throughout the TEE-COO collaborative inference.
%
\textbf{C2: Runtime Artifact Authenticity.}
The second challenge is to guarantee the authenticity of the artifacts--specifically, the data and model components--that are loaded into the trusted path at runtime.
Even though TEE provides a secure enclave, \data controls the inputs to this environment.
There's a risk that TEE may load incorrect, outdated, or even maliciously tampered data or models.
This would invalidate the trustworthiness of the trusted audit path, as the TEE would be operating on unverified artifacts.
