\myparagraph{Evaluation.}
% We extensively evaluate the effectiveness and efficiency of \method.
We extensively evaluate the performance of \method.
The evaluation covers five datasets, three models, nine metrics, and three defense baselines.
Experimental results demonstrate that when the proportion of abnormal inferences exceeds 5.4\%, \method can detect \data's abnormal execution of the inference software with a probability of 99.99\%.
In random sampling-based validation, \method achieves 100\% positive predictive value, true positive rate, and negative predictive value.
Moreover, privacy-aware training reduces the risk of input data leakage from the \data by an average of 72.9\% and only causes an average accuracy drop of 0.51\%.
\method introduces only a negligible overhead, with an average latency fluctuation of 0.56\%.
The pipeline acceleration for TEE-COO collaborative inference can reach an average of 4.38$\times$.
Finally, when the number of \data increases, the fluctuation in detection time is slight (averagely 5\%).

\myparagraph{Contributions.}
We summarize the contributions below:
\begin{itemize}
    \item To the best of our knowledge, this is the first paper to study the lack of inference auditing problem in VFL.
    \item We design \method, an inference auditing framework, to efficiently validate the execution correctness of the \data's inference software during peak inference scenarios.
    \item Extensive experimental results show \method can effectively perform detection without increasing additional system latency.
\end{itemize}

