\subsection{Vertical Federated Learning}
\label{subsec:vfl}
VFL enables collaborative model training and deployment across organizations~\cite{NguyenPPDSLDH23,QiuZJDPZW23,ZhangG22}.
It typically involves one \task and one \data, and also a third-party coordinator \coord to assist the training and inference~\cite{PangYSW23,LuoWXO21,WangG0Z23}.
\task and \data hold different feature sets ($x_t$, $x_d$) for the same users, with \task usually owning the labels $y$.
A core principle of VFL is data locality--raw features remain within each party and are never directly shared, preserving privacy by design.

\subsubsection{Comparison with Horizontal FL}
Horizontal FL (HFL) and VFL represent distinct distributed software deployment and collaboration architectures with different implications for the software lifecycle and inter-party dependencies.

In HFL, each participant holds complete feature and label data for different users.
HFL systems are engineered such that each participant's software can operate somewhat autonomously during the inference phase.
While they collaborate during a joint training phase to build a global model using their locally held, feature-complete datasets for different user sets, the inference software on each participant's side can subsequently function independently or with local personalization.
This means the software module for prediction at each HFL participant does not have a runtime dependency on other parties to make predictions, as it possesses all necessary input features.

In contrast, VFL systems are architected around a tighter coupling of software modules across participants, both for training and inference.
Participants in VFL hold different feature subsets for the same users, and the machine learning model itself is typically split across them, akin to a Split Neural Network (SplitNN)~\cite{abs-1812-00564}.
This architectural choice means that no single participant's software can independently complete the training or, crucially, the inference process.
The software modules on \task and \data must interact during a joint inference phase to produce a prediction, creating direct runtime dependencies between their respective software systems.

\subsubsection{Distributed Structure.}
The VFL system processes data through a layered, modular model structure:
\begin{itemize}
    \item Local Feature Extractor Modules.
    \task and \data each run their own bottom model software ($f_t$, $f_d$) to independently process their local data.
    \item Intermediate Representation Aggregation.
    The outputs ($h_t=f_t(x_t)$ and $h_d=f_d(x_d)$) are transmitted via defined software interfaces to \coord, which aggregates them into a joint representation $h=[h_t|h_d]$.
    \item Global Prediction Module.
    The aggregated $h$ is sent to \task, where a top model $g$ generates final predictions based on the combined features.
\end{itemize}

