\subsubsection{Pipeline.}
A typical VFL consists of three stages: preprocessing (\preprocess), joint training (\train), and joint inference (\infer). We will introduce the process of each stage as follows.

\noindent $\bullet$ \preprocess.
\task and \data perform private set intersection~\cite{FreedmanNP04} to identify overlapping sample IDs without revealing their private data.
For example, they can use encrypted names or ID numbers for alignment.
After alignment, \task partitions the overlapping samples into training, validation, and test sets.
Ground-truth labels are available to \task for training and validation, but not for the test set--these labels must be predicted using a trained VFL model.

\noindent $\bullet$ \train.
This is an iterative process involving component interactions (see Fig.~\ref{fig:splitvfl}).
\ding{172} \task initiates each training step by sending the current batch’s sample IDs to \data.
\ding{173} Both parties load local features and compute intermediate representations ($h_t$, $h_d$) via their bottom models.
\ding{174} $h_t$ and $h_d$ are securely sent to \coord, which concatenates them into a joint representation $h$ and forwards it to \task.
\ding{175} \task’s top model $g$ uses $h$ to produce predictions $\tilde y$ and compute the loss against ground-truth $y$.
\ding{176} A distributed backpropagation follows: \task computes gradients and shares relevant updates with \data, allowing all parties to update their model parameters ($f_t$, $f_d$, $g$).
This enables both \task and \data to update their respective local model parameters ($f_t$,$f_d$,$g$).
Throughout, \task monitors performance on a validation set to guide the training lifecycle and determine convergence (e.g., target accuracy or max iterations).

\noindent $\bullet$ \infer.
In deployed VFL systems, inference service typically follows a ``Pay-as-You-Go'' model.
The inference flow mirrors the training forward path (steps \ding{172}–\ding{175} in Fig.~\ref{fig:splitvfl}) but operates on unlabeled test samples.
\task initiates the process by sending test IDs to \data, often incurring a fee based on data volume.
The system then collaborates to compute aggregated representations via \coord, which \task uses with its deployed top model $g$ to produce final predictions.

