\subsection{VFL Poisoning attack and defense}
\label{subsec:vfl_poisoning}
Existing VFL security research focuses on issues related to poisoning attacks~\cite{Fu0JCWG0L022,WangLZZSRL23,YangMZKKR24,LuoWXO21,QiuZJFYW24}.
Poisoning attacks manipulate the data to decrease the overall model accuracy or implant hidden vulnerabilities into the model.
Several studies have explored detection mechanisms for VFL poisoning attacks~\cite{LaiW0LZ23,ChenZLGW24,naseri2023badvfl,BaiCZ0WG23,ChoHYLBP24}.
However, our focus is on auditing \data's local inference software in \infer.
This concern is broader than traditional poisoning, as incorrect execution may result not only from malicious actions (e.g., poisoning effects manifesting during inference) but also from various accidental software errors (e.g., incorrect model version or runtime exceptions).    
Ensuring the integrity of the inference computation itself is critical, and this paper aims to fill this gap.
