\myparagraph{Task Party's Capability.}
\task's capabilities align with prior VFL work~\cite{QiuZJDPZW23,QiuZJFYW24}.
It faithfully executes the VFL pipeline and controls $x_t$, $f_t$, and $g$, but is not permitted to directly access \data's $x_d$ or $f_d$ for auditing purposes.
\task can remotely attest the auditing program in \data's TEE.
\task does not have control over \coord and relies solely on the inference results received from \coord for auditing.


\section{\method's Design}
\label{sec:defense}
% The success of \attack attack is due to no VFL schemes offering efficient validation mechanisms to audit \data's behavior in \infer. 
% 
In this section, we will introduce our defense, \underline{Ve}rtical \underline{F}ederated \underline{I}nference \underline{A}udit framework (\method).
We first discuss the insight of our defense (Sec.~\ref{subsec:insight}), and introduce three challenges of our insight (Sec.~\ref{subsec:challenge}).
Then, we introduce three design components of \method: privacy-aware training and deployment (Sec.~\ref{subsec:training}), runtime authenticity validation (Sec.~\ref{subsec:authenticity}), and efficiency-aware computation schedule (Sec.~\ref{subsec:efficiency_schedule}).
Finally, we illustrate the workflow of \method in Sec.~\ref{subsec:workflow}.

\subsection{Insight}
\label{subsec:insight}

The privacy-efficiency-auditing contradiction refers to the challenge of auditing the correctness of the \data's inference software during large-scale inferences without compromising \data's data privacy or adding to online system latency.
\method's central insight for addressing this dilemma lies in establishing a verifiable inference audit trail by leveraging the TEE-COO framework~\cite{abs-1912-03485,mo2020darknetz}.
This approach creates dual execution paths: \data conducts its standard, high-speed (but untrusted) inference locally, while simultaneously generating a trusted result through a TEE-COO pipeline.
For this trusted path, \data's model $f_d$ is split, with privacy-sensitive shallow layers $f_d^{sm}$ executing within its TEE to protect inputs, and deeper, computationally intensive layers $f_d^{dm}$ processed by \coord.
This design is because the shallow layers are more privacy-sensitive and contain more private information of \data's data~\cite{abs-1912-03485}.
The TEE-COO mechanism ensures that raw data remains within \data's secure enclave, while \coord only receives the intermediate output $z_d = f_d^{sm}(x_d)$.
\task then audits by comparing the outputs from both the untrusted and trusted paths; discrepancies reveal potential errors or tampering by \data, thus ensuring computational integrity and accountability without compromising \data's data privacy or incurring additional online inference latency.

