Using TEE-COO trusted inference to audit \data's untrusted inference is a straightforward solution but faces several technical challenges.

\myparagraph{C1: Privacy Leak Control.}
Outsourcing model segments to \coord still risks the \data's privacy, as intermediate data $z_d$ shared with \coord can be vulnerable to model inversion attacks~\cite{HeZL19,YinZZLYCH23}.
Existing proof-of-concept methods for measuring this leakage, such as those used in DarkneTZ~\cite{mo2020darknetz}, lack rigorous guarantees against potentially stronger, unknown future attacks, necessitating a more robust approach to quantify and control information leakage.

\myparagraph{C2: Runtime Artifact Authenticity.}
\data can load incorrect data $\hat x_d$ or models $\hat f_d$ into its TEE at runtime.
This compromises the trustworthiness of TEE-COO collaborative inference because, while TEE protects computation integrity, it cannot inherently validate the authenticity of these externally supplied inputs, making the validation of inference artifacts crucial.

\myparagraph{C3: Efficient Inference Auditing.}
Auditing every untrusted inference during peak scenarios is inefficient because TEE-COO trusted inference is slower than native GPU inference.
This would significantly reduce throughput and increase service latency, thus requiring an efficient validation method to efficiently assist \task in auditing the \data's inference software.

