\subsubsection{Model Deployment.}
Following the privacy-aware training stage, the deployment of the models for the trusted and untrusted paths occurs in parallel.
For the trusted path, \data deploys the shallow subnetwork $f_d^{sm}$ along with the learned perturbation $\sigma$ into its local TEE.
The corresponding deep subnetwork $f_d^{dm}$ is uploaded to \coord to enable the secure, collaborative TEE-COO inference pipeline.
Concurrently, for the untrusted path, \data executes its full, non-instrumented inference model locally on its standard high-performance hardware.
This untrusted execution path is what \method audits for correctness by comparing its outputs against those from the trusted path.

\subsection{Runtime Authenticity Validation}
\label{subsec:authenticity}
The core challenge \method addresses here is ensuring the integrity of software components (the models) and configuration data (the input features) that are controlled by \data.
While TEE can protect the computation's integrity, it cannot inherently guarantee the authenticity of the artifacts it is instructed to load.
\data could maliciously or accidentally provide the TEE with an outdated model, an incorrectly versioned dataset, or tampered inputs, thereby compromising the entire trusted audit path.
This would render the audit meaningless, as the TEE would be operating on unverified artifacts.
Our solution is inspired by authentication and provenance-based mechanisms that provide robust and reliable defenses for critical software systems~\cite{stokes2021preventing,stokes2021preventing,baracaldo2017mitigating}.
To address \textbf{C2}, \method implements a hash consistency validation protocol that functions as a runtime assertion, validating the integrity of data and model artifacts before their use in the trusted inference path.
This protocol is executed entirely within the secure TEE enclave, making it impervious to interference from the untrusted host system controlled by \data.

% Statistical-based detection techniques that are widely used in data poisoning defenses are susceptible to false positives and false negatives~\cite{stokes2021preventing}.
% In real-world deployments, authentication and provenance-based mechanisms provide a more robust and reliable defense for ML systems~\cite{stokes2021preventing,baracaldo2017mitigating}.
% Inspired by this, we propose a hash consistency validation mechanism to check the authenticity of $x_d$ and $f_d$ to address \textbf{C2}.
% The validation process runs within the TEE, thus \data cannot interfere with the process.
% The mechanism consists of two components: data validation and model validation.

\subsubsection{Data Validation.}
This mechanism ensures the authenticity of the input data by treating it as a key configuration item whose integrity must be validated against a trusted baseline.
Before the VFL process begins (prior to \preprocess), the TEE on \data's machine computes a cryptographic hash signature of the entire dataset.
This hash, representing the ``golden version'' of the data, is then encrypted and securely managed by the TEE, making it inaccessible to \data.
This initial step is analogous to checking a verified configuration item into a secure version control system.
\data cannot tamper with the data before this step, as they have no knowledge of which samples will be used for training or inference~\cite{stokes2021preventing}; any pre-emptive tampering risks corrupting the training set and causing the model to fail performance validation, leading \task to refuse collaboration.
Therefore, existing VFL poisoning attacks are secretly implanted during \train rather than before \train~\cite{RiegerPKAKS25,BaiCZ0WG23,naseri2023badvfl}.
During \infer, for every batch of data loaded into the TEE for the trusted audit, \method re-computes its hash signature.
It then compares this runtime hash against the securely stored baseline hash.
A match confirms the data's authenticity and provenance, effectively validating that the deployed data configuration has not been altered.

