\subsubsection{Model Validation.}
This mechanism ensures that the correct, performance-validated version of the model software is being used for the trusted audit.
After the privacy-aware training and deployment process is complete, the model components ($f_d^{sm}$ and $f_d^{dm}$) are subjected to a final acceptance test.
\task provides a set of validation sample IDs, and the TEE-COO pipeline performs inference to confirm that the model's accuracy meets the pre-defined performance requirements.
This step validates the functional correctness of the trained model component.
Upon successful validation, the TEE computes a cryptographic hash of the model weights, which serves as a unique signature for this specific, approved software version.
This hash is encrypted and stored by the TEE, preventing any tampering by \data.
For each subsequent inference query that is part of the audit, \method loads the model weights into the TEE and re-computes their hash.
This hash is validated against the stored signed hash from the acceptance testing phase.
A discrepancy indicates that \data has modified or replaced the model component, perhaps with an older, buggy, or malicious version.
This triggers an immediate warning to \task, flagging a critical failure in software configuration integrity.

