\subsection{Efficiency-Aware Computation Schedule}
\label{subsec:efficiency_schedule}
A critical non-functional requirement for many online AI systems is adherence to stringent service level agreements, particularly concerning low latency and high throughput during peak service scenarios.
% \chongru{citations}
Introducing an auditing mechanism must not compromise these performance goals.
To address \textbf{C3}, we design an efficiency-aware computation schedule mechanism that enables real-time auditing without introducing observable latency into the production inference pipeline.

Due to the associated performance cost, exhaustively auditing every single inference in a high-throughput system is often infeasible.
Thus, \method adopts a statistically grounded approach to runtime validating.
Instead of a deterministic check on every inference, \method employs a confidential random sampling mechanism.
\task secretly selects a small, randomized subset of inference requests for validation via the trusted TEE-COO path.
The goal of our mechanism is not necessarily to position every abnormal inference but to detect systemic misbehavior with an extremely high degree of confidence.
% To address \textbf{C3}, our validation mechanism must be efficient and accurate enough for peak service scenarios.
% Thus, we design a confidential random sampling validation mechanism that allows \task to use TEE-COO trusted inference to validate \data's untrusted inference.
% This mechanism allows trusted and untrusted inferences to run in parallel and requires fewer trusted inferences to detect malicious behavior of \data.

Suppose $N$ samples need to be inferred in an inference query.
$K$ is the abnormal inference ratio of $N$ samples, and $W$ is the ratio of samples that \task can secretly validate in TEE.
Our key insight is that, for efficiency reasons, \task cannot audit every individual inference from \data during \infer.
A more practical and effective approach is for \method to secretly validate a designated set of $WN$ samples ($W \cdot 100\%$ of the dataset);
if any inference instance is detected to be abnormal within this set, \task can reasonably conclude that \data's inference software is executed abnormally.
This confidence level is formally defined as the Detection Success Rate (DSR), which provides a quantifiable, probabilistic guarantee of detection:
% We can formulate as follows to give a theoretical Detection Success Rate (DSR):
% $DSR=1-\binom{N-\mathit{K}N}{WN}/{\binom{N}{WN}}$.
\begin{equation}
\label{eq:prob}
% DSR=1-\frac{\binom{N-\mathit{K}N}{WN}}{\binom{N}{WN}}
DSR=1-\binom{N-\mathit{K}N}{WN}/{\binom{N}{WN}}
\end{equation}
$\binom{N}{WN}$ refers to the process of randomly sampling $WN$ instances from $N$ total inferences for validation.
$\binom{N-\mathit{K}N}{WN}$ denotes a sampling of $WN$ validation instances that does not include any abnormal inferences.
The motivation of this mechanism is that \task can secretly send the target IDs to the enclave via TEE attestation for inference validation.
Therefore, the sample IDs inferred within the enclave remain a complete black box to \data.

\subsubsection{Efficiency Optimization.}
\label{subsec:eo}
The central goal of \method is to ensure that the auditing process is latency-neutral: it should add no overhead to the online inference time experienced by the user.
\method achieves this through an optimal scheduling strategy that balances the computation time between the untrusted and trusted execution paths.
Let $T_{un}$ be the time required for \data to perform $N$ inferences using its high-speed (but untrusted) local infrastructure, and $T_{tr}$ be the time to perform the same number of inferences through the slower (but trusted) TEE-COO path.
The system will not incur additional latency if the time spent on the sampled trusted inferences equals the time of the untrusted inferences.
This leads to a resource scheduling optimization problem to find the optimal sampling ratio, $W_*$:
% To reduce latency, we aim to balance the time between trusted and untrusted inference.
% The system will not incur additional latency if the trusted and untrusted inference times are equal.
% Let $T_{un}$ and $T_{tr}$ represent the inference time for \data on $N$ samples using an untrusted accelerator and TEE-COO trusted inference, respectively.
% The optimal sampling ratio $W_{*}$ is given by:
\begin{equation}
\label{eq:osr}
    W_{*}=\myargmin_W|T_{un}-W T_{tr}|
\end{equation}
The objective is to find the optimal $W_*$ such that the total time to complete $WN$ trusted inferences (via TEE-COO; $W\times 100\%$ of the $N$ samples) equals the time required to complete $N$ untrusted samples (on GPU).
This strategy effectively masks the latency of the audit computation.
The trusted validations are scheduled to run in parallel with the main production workload, with their total execution time designed to fit within the execution time of the inference task itself.
\task can require \data to perform trusted inference on validation samples in the first model validation phase (Sec.~\ref{subsec:authenticity}) and compute $W_*$ with historical training information.
Specifically, \method formalizes the performance guarantee of the efficiency-optimal inference validation strategy:
\begin{theorem}
\label{theorem}
    (Optimal sampling auditing guarantee).
    Let an inference query consist of $N$ samples. Given an optimal sampling ratio $W_*$ and an expected defense success rate (e.g., $99.99\%$), \method guarantees that if abnormal inference rate $K$ exceeds a threshold, then the probability of detecting at least one instance of abnormal inference is not less than the expected defense success rate, and the auditing process does not introduce additional system latency.
\end{theorem}
The goal of our mechanism is to ensure that, once the number of abnormal inferences surpasses a certain threshold, \task can detect abnormal behavior from \data with high probability.
Notably, the goal is not to pinpoint individual abnormal inferences but to reliably detect the existence of anomalies.

\subsubsection{Pipeline Acceleration.}
\label{subsec:pipeline}
The TEE enclave, while ensuring strong execution integrity, offers limited computational capacity and memory (e.g., constrained by the size of the Enclave Page Cache, EPC).
In contrast, the coordinator--typically hosted in a data center—possesses abundant GPU-based resources.
In a naive implementation of the TEE-COO collaborative inference workflow, \coord must wait until all TEE-side operations are complete before it can begin its own computation.
This introduces an idle wait period and underutilizes available resources, violating the design principle of resource concurrency.

To optimize end-to-end latency and enable concurrent utilization of both TEE and coordinator resources, \method adopts a block-based pipeline execution strategy.
This design partitions the inference workload into multiple execution blocks, allowing different pipeline stages (TEE computation, secure communication, coordinator inference) to overlap in time.
The goal is to minimize the completion time of the last operation in the pipeline while respecting memory and scheduling constraints.
The collaborative inference pipeline is composed of the following sequential operations:
\begin{itemize}
    \item $o_{tee}$: Secure computation in the TEE using shallow model $f_d^{sm}$.
    \item $o_{comm}$: Communication of intermediate outputs (e.g., perturbed representations) to \coord.
    \item $o_{coo}$: Coordinator-side inference using the deep model $f_d^{dm}$.
\end{itemize}
Each pipeline block $B_i$ is assigned a subset of inference samples and progresses through these operations in sequence.
By overlapping the execution of $B_i$ and $B_{i+1}$ across different operations, the system can exploit temporal parallelism, significantly reducing idle time.
To ensure feasibility, pipeline execution must satisfy:
\begin{itemize}
    \item EPC constraint: The size of each block must fit within the EPC limit.
    \item Operation dependency: Later operations (e.g., communication, coordinator-side inference) can only begin after their predecessors complete within the same block.
    \item Resource exclusivity: A single operation can process only one block at a time, enforcing mutual exclusion per resource unit.
\end{itemize}
% \method's trusted inference relies on collaborative inference between the \data's TEE and \coord's GPU.
% \coord needs to wait until the local enclave completes inference on all samples.
% In scenarios with a large number of model parameters, the computing power of SGX will become a bottleneck, causing \coord to be idle for too long.
% Thus we use a block inference pipeline execution to accelerate collaborative inference and improve the system efficiency.
% Trusted inference consists of three operations $o=\{o_{sgx},o_{comm},o_{coo}\}$, which correspond to the SGX computation, the TEE-COO communication, and the \coord computation, respectively.
% During the first model validation, \method accurately evaluates the TEE-COO inference latency $T_o$ of three operations for the determination of the optimal number of blocks $B_*$.
% To avoid complex combinations, we set $o_{comm}$ to a constant~\cite{Jiang0C24}.
We transform this process into an optimization problem, specifically targeting the minimization of the completion time $\beta^{end}_{O,B}$ for the final operation $O$ of the last block $B$:
\begin{align}
B_{*} &= \myargmin_{B} \beta^{end}_{O,B} \label{eq:optimal_block} \\ 
\text{s.t.} \quad  \beta^{end}_{o,b} &= \beta^{start}_{o,b} + T_o\\ B + f_d^{sm} &\leq EPC \label{eq:epc} \\
{order}_{o,b} &= \mathbb{I}\,({o\ne0}) \cdot \beta^{end}_{o-1,b} \label{eq:order} \\
{resource}_{o,b} &= \mathbb{I}\,{((o,b) \neq (0,0))} \cdot \beta^{end}_{o,b-1} \label{eq:resource}
\end{align}
%
% \ziqi{refine equation}
% \begin{align}
%     B^{*}&=\myargmin_{B} \beta^{end}_{O,B} \notag \\
%     st.\quad \beta^{end}_{o,b}&=\beta^{start}_{o,b}+T_o \notag \\
%     {order}_{o,b}&=\begin{cases}
%         0,\quad\quad\quad\, o=0\\
%         \beta^{end}_{o-1,b},\quad o\ne0
%     \end{cases}\label{eq:order}\\
%     {resource}_{o,b}&=\begin{cases}
%         0,\quad\quad\quad o=0\, \&\, b=0\\
%         \beta^{end}_{o,b-1},\quad otherwise
%     \end{cases}\label{eq:resource}\\
%     B+f_d^{sm}&\le EPC \label{eq:epc}
