\section{Evaluation}
This section evaluates the performance of \method.
We first describe the setup (Sec.~\ref{subsec:defense_setup}), followed by the questions that need to be experimentally verified (Sec.~\ref{subsec:question}),
differences between theoretical and empirical validation (Sec.~\ref{subsec:main_eval}), 
detection effectiveness (Sec.\ref{subsec:defense_eval}),
privacy protection results (Sec.~\ref{subsec:privacy_eval}), system efficiency on real hardware (Sec.~\ref{subsec:system_efficiency}), and scalability with increasing numbers of \data (Sec.~\ref{subsec:scalability}).

\subsection{Evaluation Setup}
\label{subsec:defense_setup}

\subsubsection{Implementation.} 
% We implemented \method on the same platform as Sec.~\ref{subsec:attack_eval_setup}.
% Our framework consists of two parts: a TEE part implemented based on SGX and a COO part implemented based on a GPU.
We implement the prototype of \method and evaluate the performance on a server with NVIDIA RTX 4070ti super, Intel 9700KF, 32GB of memory, PyTorch 2.1, and CUDA driver 12.0.
The SGX module is developed in C++ using the Eigen 3.2.10 library and compiled by Intel SGX SDK 2.23 and GCC 11.4.0.
We provide the artifact at \url{https://anonymous.4open.science/r/VeFIA}.

\subsubsection{Datasets.}
We conduct the experiments on five representative datasets: Bank Marketing (BM)~\cite{MoroCR14}, Credit Card Fraud Detection (CCFD)~\cite{YehL09a}, Medical-MNIST (MMNIST)~\cite{medicalmnist}, CIFAR10~\cite{krizhevsky2009learning}, and KDD-CUP~\cite{TavallaeeBLG09}.
The choice of datasets follows the common practice of prior VFL studies~\cite{HuangW023,LuoWXO21,naseri2023badvfl}.
BM has 82,376 samples, 63 features, and 2 classes.
CCFD has 569,614 samples, 29 features, and two classes. 
MMNIST has 58,954 medical images (64$\times$64) of 6 classes, and CIFAR10 has 50,000 images (3$\times$32$\times$32) of 10 classes.
KDD-CUP is a large-scale tabular dataset with 4,898,431 samples, 33 features, and 5 classes.
Common VFL datasets (e.g. BM, CCFD, MMNIST, and CIFAR10 datasets) typically contain tens of thousands of samples, whereas the KDD-CUP dataset reaches a million-scale magnitude (10 to 100 times larger).
Thus, KDD-CUP dataset is used to evaluate the efficiency (Sec.~\ref{subsec:system_efficiency}) and scalability (Sec.~\ref{subsec:scalability}) of \method.
Tbl.~\ref{tab:dataset} shows the feature split rule.
Recall that in Sec.~\ref{subsec:vfl}, we consider one $\mathcal{P}_t$ and one $\mathcal{P}_d$.
Therefore, we divide the data into two parts along the feature dimension and hold them in two parties.

