\subsubsection{Metrics.} 
% We used the three metrics mentioned in Sec.~\ref{subsec:attack_eval_setup} (PPV, TPR, and NPV), along with four new metrics:
% We follow prior work and use five metrics to evaluate the attack performance~\cite{ChoHYLBP24,KumariRFJS23,LiD24,NguyenRCYMFMMMZ22}: Accuracy, Attack Success Rate (ASR), Positive Predictive Value (PPV), True Positive Rate (TPR), and Negative Predictive Value (NPV).
% We use accuracy and ASR to evaluate the attack performance and use the other three metrics to evaluate the defense effectiveness of prior defenses.
% These evaluation metrics are described in detail in Appx.~\ref{app:eval_metrics}.
% For PPV, TPR, and NPV, a higher value indicates a better defense performance.
We used the following five metrics to evaluate \method.
\begin{itemize}
    \item \textit{Positive Predictive Value (PPV)} measures the proportion of truly abnormal inferences among those identified as abnormal by the detection mechanism.
    True Positives (TP) represents the number of correctly identified abnormal inferences.
    False Positive (FP) is the number of normal inferences incorrectly identified as abnormal.
    PPV is computed as $PPV=\frac{TP}{TP+FP}$.
    \item \textit{True Positive Rate (TPR)} measures the proportion of correctly identified abnormal inferences among all abnormal inferences.
    Let False Negatives (FN) represent the number of abnormal inferences that are incorrectly identified as normal.
    TPR is computed as $TPR=\frac{TP}{TP+FN}$.
    \item \textit{Negative Predictive Value (NPV)} measures the proportion of correctly identified normal inferences to all normal inferences.
    True Negatives (TN) is the total number of correctly identified normal inferences.
    NPV is computed as $NPV=\frac{TN}{TN+FN}$.
    \item \textit{Detection Success Rate (DSR)}:
    The probability of detecting an abnormal inference in \method's random sampling validation (Eq.~\ref{eq:prob}).
    \item \textit{Speedup ratio}:
    Speed ratio is the inference latency improvement of \method (w/o pipeline acceleration) over \method.
    % A higher value indicates lower overall system latency, demonstrating the efficiency gains achieved through pipeline optimization.
\end{itemize}
For PPV, TPR, NPV, DSR, and speed ratio, a higher value indicates a better detection performance.
Following prior work~\cite{LiuF00024,abs-2404-15821}, we evaluate the similarity between original and inversed data using Hitting Rate (HR) for tabular data (BM and CCFD) and Structural Similarity Index Measure (SSIM) for image data (MMNIST and CIFAR10).
We adopt safety thresholds of 0.09 for HR and 0.3 for SSIM~\cite{abs-2404-15821,HeZL19}, below which inversion is considered unsuccessful.

\subsubsection{Detection Baselines.}
\label{subsec:baseline}
We choose three state-of-the-art poisoning defenses in VFL as baselines: VFedAD~\cite{LaiW0LZ23}, P-GAN~\cite{ChenZLGW24}, and VFLIP~\cite{ChoHYLBP24}.
These defenses rely on using deep learning techniques (e.g., contrastive learning~\cite{abs-1807-03748}, masked auto-encoder~\cite{HeCXLDG22}) to perform anomaly detection on the statistical properties of intermediate representations (e.g., $h_d$).

\subsubsection{Abnormal Inference Setup.}
\label{subsec:abnormal_inference}
By default, we set half of the inferences ($K=50\%$) to be abnormal.
We use two types of methods to simulate abnormal execution of the \data's inference software.
The first is a malicious scenario, where we use state-of-the-art poisoning and backdoor attacks against VFL.
In line with the literature~\cite{ChoHYLBP24}, we set the poisoning budget to 50\% and the target label to 0.
\begin{itemize}
    \item BadVFL~\cite{naseri2023badvfl}.
    For BadVFL, the attack first involves a label inference phase where the adversary reconstructs labels for its data samples, often using auxiliary data to train a surrogate model.
    Then, in the backdoor phase, the attacker selects source and target classes, injects a trigger into some of its data samples, and submits feature embeddings designed to make these triggered samples appear close to the target class in the embedding space.
    \item VILLAIN~\cite{BaiCZ0WG23}.
    For VILLAIN, the attack process also begins with a label inference module, which deduces if a sample belongs to the target misclassification label using techniques like ``embedding swapping'' based on gradient changes and candidate selection.
    Following this, the data poisoning module fabricates poisoned embedding vectors by applying a stealthy additive trigger directly to the embeddings of target-label samples and employs backdoor augmentation strategies to inject the backdoor into the \task's model.
\end{itemize}
The second scenario considers accidental misbehavior.
For models, we either injected subtle random noise (less 1e-6) or used checkpoints from earlier training epochs.
For tabular data, we unintentionally replaced feature values, while for image data, we also introduced subtle random noise (less 1e-6).
We use the second scenario for evaluation by default.

\subsubsection{Privacy Evaluation Setup.}
We use model inversion attacks to evaluate our privacy-aware training mechanism and how much \method can protect the input privacy of \data.
Specifically, we select four state-of-the-art inversion attacks for \coord to reconstruct the inputs $x_d$ of the shallow model $f_d^{sm}$:
% (details in Appx.~\ref{app:moia}): 
query-free model inversion~\cite{HeZL19}, Genver~\cite{YinZZLYCH23}, UIFV~\cite{abs-2406-12588}, and FIA~\cite{LuoWXO21}.
The settings of these attacks are consistent with the original paper.
\begin{itemize}
    \item Query-free model inversion attack.
    He et al.~\cite{HeZL19} first introduced model inversion attacks, which enable the reconstruction of \data's original data in query-free collaborative inference.
    Query-free setting leverages a shadow dataset to train a surrogate model that approximates the functionality of $f_d^{sm}$.
    Regularized maximum likelihood estimation is then applied to the surrogate model to recover sensitive inputs.
    % Subsequently, it employs regularized maximum likelihood estimation on the surrogate model to recover sensitive inputs.
    \item Ginver~\cite{YinZZLYCH23}.
    Compared to the query-free model inversion attack, Ginver needs a stronger attacker.
    In this setting, \coord can query $f_d^{sm}$ using a shadow dataset and then train an inversion model $f^{-1}$ to reconstruct the original data.
    \item UIFV~\cite{abs-2406-12588}.
    UIFV is another query-based attack that utilizes a data generator to synthesize a set of fake samples, which are then used to query $f_d^{sm}$ and extract intermediate features.
    Subsequently, UIFV trains an inversion model to reconstruct the original data.
    \item FIA~\cite{LuoWXO21}.
    FIA assumes that the attacker has access to the parameters of $f_d^{sm}$ and uses the generative regression network along with the maximum a posteriori estimation method to infer the inputs of \data.
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Research Questions.}
\label{subsec:question}
We evaluate \method\ on the following questions:

\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{RQ1.} Whether the practical detection performance of \method is consistent with its theoretical guarantee (Eq.~\ref{eq:prob}).
    \item \textbf{RQ2.} How does \method detect different abnormal inference scenarios compared to existing baselines.
    \item \textbf{RQ3.} What is the trade-off between privacy protection and model utility of \method?
    \item \textbf{RQ4.} What impact will the deployment of VeFIA have on real system efficiency?
    \item \textbf{RQ5.} Does \method's auditing performance degrade as the number of participants grows?
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Theoretical vs Empirical Validation}
\label{subsec:main_eval}
\method guarantees a high detection success rate DSR (e.g., $99.99\%$) when the abnormal inference rate $K$ exceeds a certain threshold.
Eq.~\ref{eq:prob} defines this theoretical threshold for $K$.
This experiment aims to verify whether DSR approaches the target level when $K$ is near this theoretical threshold.

According to Sec.~\ref{subsec:efficiency_schedule}, \method can use the validation samples during the first model validation to determine the optimal $W_*$.
Under optimal $W_*$ (column $W_*$ in Tbl.~\ref{tab:theoretical}), using \method for inference auditing does not introduce additional latency to the online inference system.
The $W_*$ for BM and CCFD are higher than those for MMNIST and CIFAR10 due to the greater computational overhead of the convolutional layers compared to the linear layers.

According to Thm.~\ref{theorem}, $W_*$ can achieve the expected DSR (e.g. 90\% or 99.99\%) when the abnormal inference rate $K$ exceeds a certain value.
Using Eq.~\ref{eq:prob}, we compute a theoretical lower bound for $K$.
In Tbl.~\ref{tab:theoretical}, we further validate whether the real $K$ aligns with the theoretical lower bound.
Column $T$ in Tbl.\ref{tab:theoretical} represents the theoretical lower bound derived from Eq.~\ref{eq:prob}, indicating that as long as $K$ exceeds this bound, \method can detect abnormal inferences by \data with a DSR of 90\% or 99.99\%.
In our experiment, we iteratively decreased the lower bound (in steps of $1\%$) to determine whether the actual DSR met the expected DSR.
The results in Tbl.~\ref{tab:theoretical} demonstrate that \method guarantees that abnormal behavior can be detected with a probability of 99.99\% when $K$ exceeds an average threshold of 5.4\% in practice.
Furthermore, as shown in Tbl.~\ref{tab:theoretical}, the real values $K$ (Column $K_r$) across all four datasets are consistently lower than the theoretical lower bound (Column $K_t$).
This indicates that, in practical scenarios, \method can achieve the desired DSR for the \task with a smaller $K$ than theoretically required.

