\subsection{Privacy Protection}
\label{subsec:privacy_eval}

\method works by having a trusted TEE-COO pipeline run in parallel with the normal inference.
In this trusted path, part of the \data's model computation is outsourced to \coord.
This creates a new potential privacy risk: the intermediate data sent to \coord, while not the raw input, could still be used to infer or reconstruct the original private information (e.g., through model inversion attacks).
Therefore, \method must incorporate strong privacy protection to prevent this leakage and ensure the audit itself doesn't become a security vulnerability.
To assess the effectiveness of our privacy-aware training mechanism (Sec.~\ref{subsec:training}) in the context of outsourced inference, we conduct a comprehensive evaluation using four representative privacy inference attacks.
Specifically, we use HR to evaluate BM and CCFD datasets and use SSIM for MMNIST and CIFAR-10 datasets.

As reported in Tbl.~\ref{tab:eval_privacy}, our approach, \method, consistently maintains HR and SSIM scores below the empirically established safety thresholds of 0.09 and 0.3, respectively.
This indicates a strong resistance to privacy leakage across diverse data modalities.
On average, \method reduces the similarity between original inputs and reconstructed data by 72.9\%, demonstrating its efficacy in mitigating information exposure risks.

Moreover, the incorporation of privacy-aware training incurs only a marginal 0.51\% average reduction in inference accuracy, as shown in Tbl.~\ref{tab:accuracy}.
This minimal trade-off highlights that \method achieves robust privacy protection without significantly degrading model utility.
Taken together, these results affirm the practical viability of \method in preserving data confidentiality while maintaining high-performance inference.

