\subsection{Scalability to Multi-Party VFL System}
\label{subsec:scalability}

One potential concern regarding the practicality of \method is its scalability with respect to the number of participating entities.
While the standard VFL setup typically involves one data party \data and one task performer \task (Sec.~\ref{subsec:vfl}), real-world deployments may involve multiple \data parties collaborating simultaneously.
To evaluate the scalability of \method under such settings, we vary the number of \data participants from 3 to 9 (specifically, 3, 5, 7, and 9) and measure the corresponding system latency overhead.
For each experiment, we randomly assign a $K$ between 1\% and 100\% to each \data to simulate the inference execution.
We maintain a fixed detection configuration using the optimal threshold $W_*$ across all runs.
Tbl.~\ref{tab:scalability} presents the system latency relative to the baseline single-party case (i.e., one \data).
Our results show that the average detection latency increases by only 5\% as the number of participating \data grows--an increment that is marginal in practical terms.
This empirical evidence suggests that \method introduces minimal additional cost even in collaborative, multi-party VFL settings.
Overall, these findings demonstrate that \method is highly scalable and can be seamlessly extended to realistic deployments involving many \data participants.
