\section{Discussion}
\label{sec:discussion}
\myparagraph{Target Scenarios.}
\method enables \task to audit whether the inferences of \data are executed as expected, without inducing additional latency.
\method is not to audit every single inference, but to treat a batch of inferences as a single transactional unit of work.
The goal is to determine if any anomalies exist within that batch, which is more aligned with real-world software monitoring where the focus is on detecting systemic failures rather than isolated, transient errors~\cite{zhao2021veriml}.
By employing a statistically-grounded random sampling strategy, \method provides a probabilistic guarantee of anomaly detection.
As demonstrated in experiments, \method can achieve a 99.99\% detection success rate once the proportion of abnormal inferences surpasses a small threshold (e.g., 5.4\%).
% \method allows for the validation of a small subset of inferences while ensuring that, if the abnormal inferences of \data exceed a certain threshold, \task has an extremely high probability of detecting the abnormal behavior of \data.
% In our experiments, we demonstrate that \method achieves a high detection success rate (DSR = 99.99\%) in identifying abnormal inferences, provided that the total number of inferences exceeds a relatively small threshold (averaging $5.4\%$).
% In essence, \method is designed to prevent \data from manipulating a substantial portion of inferences without \task's awareness.
% Therefore, compared to validating each inference, \method is more suitable for high-throughput, low-latency online inference systems.

\myparagraph{Real-World Deployments.}
Our current implementation of \method is based on Intel SGX.
However, \method can be easily extended to other TEE hardware platforms, such as ARM CCA~\cite{arm2024cca}, AMD SEV~\cite{morbitzer2018severed}.
It is because \method relies on the confidentiality and integrity guarantees provided by the TEE runtime rather than on specific hardware infrastructure.
As a result, \method can be rapidly deployed and effectively perform auditing, even in VFL inference tasks involving heterogeneous systems among VFL participants.
In our experiments, we use the Intel i7-9700KF CPU, which supports an EPC size of up to 128MB (without considering page swapping) for SGX.
Existing server-level Intel Xeon processors provide an EPC size of up to 512GB and additional cores~\cite{intel-sgx-support}, enabling inference auditing for models with larger parameter sizes.
In VFL settings, the participants are usually enterprises or institutions with richer computing resources, making TEE-based deployment a practical and feasible solution.

\myparagraph{Impact on Numerical Precision.}
The transition between TEE-COO computing environments can introduce variations in numerical precision.
For instance, the same data may be subject to different precision truncation in SGX and GPU environments.
\method's data/model validation and inference consistency verification rely on hash computations, which may be sensitive to minor numerical discrepancies.
As a result, small changes in precision can lead to differing hash values for the same inputs.
To ensure \method's inference auditing is reliable, the validation results are lossless--i.e., any detected inconsistency must originate from abnormal inferences by \data, rather than benign variations in numerical precision.
The results in Tbl.~\ref{tab:vefia_defense} show that \method achieves a PPV of $100\%$, indicating all inferences flagged as abnormal are true positives, with no false positives.
This result validates that \method provides effective and reliable detection without compromising numerical precision.

\myparagraph{Future Work.}
Building on this work, we propose the following directions for future work to further advance reliable and secure collaborative AI software:
\begin{enumerate}
    \item More application scenario adaptation.
    Our current work primarily considers FinTech scenarios where the coordinator \coord (usually the regulatory authority) can often be assumed to be neutral.
    Future research will explore adapting \method to other domains like healthcare~\cite{HuangW023,abs-2501-11388} and recommendation systems~\cite{WangWHPYYWF25,0008WWXZCFCL24}.
    These domains may present different regulatory landscapes and trust assumptions, necessitating the design of robust software auditing mechanisms that can operate effectively with a less trusted \coord.
    \item End-to-end auditable VFL lifecycle.
    \method currently focuses on the inference phase (\infer).
    Future work should aim to extend our paradigm across the entire VFL lifecycle.
    This includes developing mechanisms to audit the integrity and correctness of \preprocess and \train, providing a comprehensive, end-to-end trust guarantee for VFL systems.
    \item From software anomaly detection to automated resilience.
    At present, \method's primary role is to detect and flag inconsistencies.
    A significant next step would be to build automated anomaly response and system resilience capabilities.
    This could involve designing protocols that, upon detecting an anomaly, can automatically isolate the non-compliant party, roll back to a last-known-good state, or trigger a more intensive diagnostic audit, thereby moving from simple detection to building self-healing, resilient AI systems.
\end{enumerate}
