\section{Related Work}

\subsection{FL for Software Engineering.}
FL is increasingly being explored for various software engineering tasks, aiming to leverage data from multiple sources without direct data sharing.
Existing research has demonstrated its potential in areas like code clone detection, defect prediction, commit classification, and code summarization.
For instance, Yang et al.~\cite{YangHGCNXL24} proposed ALMITY, a FL framework designed to improve model training for code clone detection and defect prediction, particularly addressing data imbalance and heterogeneity.
Studies have also shown that FL can achieve performance comparable to centralized learning in tasks like commit classification and code summarization, even when dealing with non-open source project data~\cite{ShanbhagC22,KumarC24}.
Furthermore, FL has been integrated with systematic debugging for fault localization~\cite{GillAG23}.

\myparagraph{Our Contribution.}
While these studies explore FL for training models in software engineering, our work, \method, addresses a different but complementary challenge: ensuring the trustworthiness and correctness of the inference phase in VFL deployments.
Unlike the existing research that primarily focuses on model training, privacy in training, or specific software engineering tasks like defect prediction or code summarization, \method introduces a framework to audit the execution correctness of the \data's local inference software.
This is crucial for accountability in collaborative AI software, particularly in high-stakes domains where incorrect inference can have significant consequences. To our knowledge, \method is the first to systematically address the inference auditing problem in the VFL for software engineering applications.

\subsection{TEE for FL.}
PPFL utilizes TEE on both client training and server aggregation against attacks on gradients and models~\cite{MoHKMPK21}.
TrustFL~\cite{ZhangLZLWW20} uses TEE to randomly check a portion of the training process and output a model update with a signature as proof.
Olive uses TEE to implement model aggregation on untrusted servers and designs a new aggregation algorithm to prevent memory access pattern leakage~\cite{Kato0Y23}.
Some other studies~\cite{ZhangWCHM21,ZhaoJFWSL22} have also shown that TEE-enabled FL has great potential.
These studies demonstrate the significant potential of TEE-enabled FL, but their primary focus remains on the HFL paradigm.
% However, these works only consider HFL.
% This paper focuses on how to leverage TEE in VFL to audit the trustworthiness of the \data's inference software.

\myparagraph{Our Contribution.}
In contrast to previous works that concentrate on HFL, \method is the first to leverage TEE to address the distinct challenges present in VFL.
Our framework's focus is not on securing the training phase, such as protecting gradients or model aggregation, but on ensuring the correctness and accountability of the inference phase.
\method introduces a novel application of TEE to audit the trustworthiness of the \data's inference software, a critical requirement in the VFL collaborative deployment model that is not addressed by existing TEE-for-HFL frameworks.


% \subsection{TEE-based secure inference.}
% TEE-based secure inference generally falls into two categories.
% The first, TEE-shielding approaches~\cite{Hanzlik0G0A0F21,LeeLPLLLXXZS19}, execute the entire inference process within a secure enclave.
% The second type~\cite{ShenQJWWCZWCLZC22,abs-1912-03485} is the partitioning-based method, which executes a portion of model layers within the secure enclave.
% However, these methods can only guarantee the inference integrity but cannot ensure the authenticity of the data and model.

\subsection{VFL poisoning detection.}
Existing VFL research focuses on the impact of harmful data and models, such as poisoning attacks and backdoor attacks~\cite{naseri2023badvfl,BaiCZ0WG23}, and also proposes detection mechanisms for these attacks~\cite{LaiW0LZ23,ChoHYLBP24,ChenZLGW24}.
Chen et al.~\cite{ChenZLGW24} uses a deep auto-encoder deployed on \task to detect and filter out poisoned data.
The auto-encoder reconstructs the embedding vectors received from \data and identifies anomalies based on reconstruction error--vectors with high reconstruction error.
VFLIP~\cite{ChoHYLBP24} uses a masked auto-encoder to identify backdoor-triggered embeddings through participant-wise anomaly detection with majority voting.
VFedAD~\cite{LaiW0LZ23} identifies anomalies by learning robust data representations through a contrastive learning task, to maximize shared information, and a cross-client prediction task, to discard irrelevant information.
% Existing mechanisms only focus on scenarios where \data is malicious.
% In contrast, \method is a more general detection mechanism that not only defends against malicious \data, but also detects the accidental use of the wrong software version.

\myparagraph{Our Contribution.}
\method offers a more general detection framework that extends beyond the scope of traditional poisoning detection.
While existing mechanisms are tailored to scenarios where \data is malicious, \method is designed to detect a broader spectrum of abnormal executions.
This includes not only deliberate poisoning attacks but also accidental software errors, such as the use of a wrong model version or runtime exceptions that lead to incorrect results.
By focusing on the consistency of inference results, \method provides a mechanism for overall software execution accountability, which is a wider and more fundamental concern from a software engineering perspective than defending against a single class of attack.

\subsection{FL Auditing.}
Chang et al. propose novel and efficient membership inference attacks for auditing privacy risks in HFL~\cite{ChangEPS24}.
Li et al. introduce a new attack, generative gradient leakage, which can effectively reconstruct private training data from shared gradients in HFL~\cite{Li0L022}.
Furthermore, they use this attack as a privacy auditing tool in HFL, helping to design future privacy mechanisms by demonstrating the extent of information leakage under different defense settings.
Zhang et al. propose a novel FL framework that incorporates a data integrity auditing function to evaluate the reputation of each participant~\cite{ZhangL24}.
These FL audit frameworks primarily focus on HFL and aim to protect participant privacy during the training phase~\cite{ChangEPS24,ZhangL24}.
% In contrast, our work targets the joint inference phase of VFL, ensuring that the inference software of \data is executed correctly through inference consistency audit.

\myparagraph{Our Contribution.}
\method introduces a novel auditing paradigm that is distinct from existing work on two key axes: the learning setting (VFL vs. HFL) and the software lifecycle stage (inference vs. training).
Our work targets the joint inference phase of VFL, and the goal is not to audit for privacy leaks or participant reputation, but to ensure the correct execution of the deployed inference software.
\method's inference consistency audit provides a verifiable mechanism to ensure accountability and correctness in the collaborative deployment of VFL software, addressing a critical gap not covered by existing FL auditing frameworks.

\section{Conclusion}
This paper addressed a critical accountability gap in VFL, a distributed AI software architecture for cross-silo collaboration.
We identified that \task lacks a mechanism to verify the correct execution of the \data's local inference software, creating significant risks in high-stakes applications.
To solve this, we designed and implemented \method, a novel framework that provides verifiable inference auditing for VFL systems.
By creating parallel trusted and untrusted execution paths and employing a sampling-grounded auditing strategy, \method successfully navigates the complex trade-offs between three critical non-functional requirements: security (by ensuring computational integrity), privacy (by protecting the \data's inputs), and efficiency (by introducing negligible online latency).
Our empirical evaluation demonstrates that \method can detect abnormal inference execution with over 99.99\% probability once tampering exceeds a minimal threshold of 5.4\%, thereby establishing a strong foundation for trust and accountability between collaborating parties in a VFL deployment.
