\subsection{Network Architecture}
\label{sec:architecture}

\noindent Our proposed network preserves the encoder–decoder macro‐structure of the baseline while threading edge information through every decoding step. Given an RGB image $\mathbf{I}\in\mathbb{R}^{3\times H\times W}$, a ResNet‐34 backbone pretrained on ImageNet produces five spatial‐scale feature maps $\{\mathbf{e}_1,\mathbf{e}_2,\ldots,\mathbf{e}_5\}$ at strides $\{2,4,8,16,32\}$, respectively. Starting from $\mathbf{E}_5$, a symmetric decoder comprising five upsampling blocks restores the original resolution. At every scale $i\in\{4,3,2,1\}$, except the coarsest, we insert the W-EGA module (see Sec.~\ref{fig:architecture}), replacing the Laplacian‐based EGA used in the MEGANet baseline. Each decoder stage $\mathbf{d}_i$ is projected to a side output $\mathbf{P}_i$ via a $3\times3$ Conv–BN–ReLU block, followed by a $1\times1$ Conv prediction head. The side logits are bilinearly resized to the input resolution and jointly supervised. Table~\ref{tab:feature_dims} summarizes the feature tensor dimensions across MEGANet-W.\\

