A proxy to find the best-fit parameters $\theta'$ given a dataset is to maximize the matched signal-to-noise ratio (SNR), given by:
\begin{equation}
\label{eq:SNR}
    \rho = \frac{\langle d, s(\theta') \rangle}{\sqrt{\langle s(\theta'), s(\theta') \rangle}},
\end{equation}
which is independent of the luminosity distance $D_L$. The optimal estimate for $D_L$ maximizing the likelihood is:
\begin{equation}
\label{eq:maxD}
    D_L = \frac{\langle s(\theta'), s(\theta') \rangle}{\langle d, s(\theta') \rangle},
\end{equation}

where $\theta' = \theta \setminus \{ D_L \}$. The optimal SNR is defined as:

\begin{equation}
    \rho_{\text{optimal}} \coloneqq \sqrt{\langle s(\theta), s(\theta) \rangle},
\end{equation}

which differs from the detected SNR due to noise fluctuations.

