 Cells respond to environmental changes by regulating various signaling pathways, which modify their shape, size, and behavior in response to the signals~\cite{lim2014cell}. In a broad class of cellular systems, the response is carried out by coupling the cell mechanics to cellular chemistry through mechanochemical feedback loops (MCFLs)~\cite{gross2017active,hannezo_mechanochemical_2019,bruckner_tissue_2024,ioratim-uba_mechanochemical_2023,lan2015biomechanical,banerjee2015propagating,sknepnek2023generating,dierkes2014spontaneous,staddon2019mechanosensitive,cavanaugh2020adaptive,etournay2015interplay,noll2017active,saw2017topological,streichan2018global,ibrahimi2023deforming,clement2017viscoelastic,khalilgharibi2019stress,wurthner2023geometry,banerjee2024hydrodynamics,kaouri2017mathematical,sartori2019effect,rautu2024active,dhanuka2025excitability}. MCFLs arise generically through the crosstalk of various cell signaling networks~\cite{hannezo_mechanochemical_2019,de2022interplay,lim2014cell}. A striking example of which is realized in epithelial tissues~\cite{boocock_interplay_2023,boocock_theory_2021,perez-verdugo_excitable_2024,parmar2025spontaneous,shankaran_rapid_2009,moreno_competition_2019,hino2020erk}. The mitogen-activated protein kinase ERK regulates the actin cytoskeleton through its many downstream effectors~\cite{barros2005activation,logue2015erk,lavoie2020erk,mendoza2011erk,mendoza2015erk,tanimura2017erk,de2021membrane,de2022interplay} and it itself is regulated by the activation of its upstream epidermal growth factor receptors (EGFR) through mechanical stretching of the cell~\cite{hino2020erk,boocock_theory_2021,boocock_interplay_2023,perez-verdugo_excitable_2024}. Hence, the dynamics of the actin cytoskeleton and ERK are intimately coupled to each other. 

 In single cells, ERK shows relaxation oscillations~\cite{kholodenko2010signalling,arkun2018dynamics}. In migrating epithelial tissues, the coupling of ERK oscillation to the mechanical stresses generates traveling pulses of ERK~\cite{hino2020erk,aoki2017propagating}. In contrast, in compressed or confluent tissues, ERK oscillation is suppressed and ERK is degraded~\cite{shankaran_rapid_2009,moreno_competition_2019}. This transition is critical in Drosophila pupal notum development, where the suppression of ERK oscillation through cell compaction triggers pattern formation through apoptosis~\cite{moreno_competition_2019}. This transition from an oscillatory to nonoscillatory state is reminiscent of Hopf bifurcation, driven here by the changes in the mechanical stresses. Indeed, in single cells, ERK can undergo Hopf bifurcation~\cite{arkun2018dynamics,kholodenko2010signalling}. However, the underlying chemical system is so complex~\cite{kholodenko2010signalling} that drawing general conclusions about the observed transition is nearly impossible. Specifically, it remains unclear whether the compaction-driven oscillation death (COD) is a generic feature of a mechanochemically coupled (MCC) Hopf oscillators or whether it arises due to the specific biochemistry governing ERK dynamics.   

To resolve this issue, in this paper, we study a MCC 1D periodic continuum cellular vertex model, which we term Harmonic Brusselator Ring (HBR), an active solid. In HBR, each cell's mechanical state, such as its preferred length $L_0$, is coupled to the chemical state of a Brusselator. Brusselator is a simple and generic Hopf oscillator and has been used to model a wide variety of biological patterns~\cite{fall_biochemical_2004,epstein1998introduction}. Here, we show that COD is generically produced in HBR. Intriguingly, collective behaviors of coupled Hopf oscillators in 1D rings, such as HBR, are governed only by the underlying symmetry of the coupling and not by the specific realizations of the Hopf oscillators~\cite{collins_coupled_1993,collins_group-theoretic_1994,epstein_symmetric_1993}, which establishes our results for a broader class of MCC Hopf oscillators. Finally, we show that COD in HBR arises due to spontaneously broken $\mathcal{PT}$-symmetry. Thus, we demonstrate that COD arises generically in MCC Hopf oscillators. 

Due to the underlying non-Hermiticity of HBR and the associated broken $\mathcal{PT}$-symmetry, we observe nontrivial patterns, such as spatially localized traveling waves, clustered oscillation death, chimera states, and ephemeral, local, \& evanescent traveling waves as the coupling strength, $\mu$, is varied (see SI movies). 
%Many of these patterns originate from the non-Hermiticity of the model and the associated broken $\mathcal{PT}$-symmetry. 
We analyze this system at various levels of spatiotemporal organization and show that the generated patterns arise through three dynamical phase transitions~\cite{international_centre_for_theoretical_sciences_dynamical_2019}.

