\paragraph*{Single cell model:} A single cell, in the absence of any MCC, is described by the following equations:
\begin{geqn}
    \del_t M = a -(b+1)M + cM^2E \\
    \del_t E = bM - cM^2E - DE\\ 
    \tau_D\del_t D = -(D-D_0)
    \label{Eq:Bruss1}
\end{geqn}
Here, $a,b,c$ are the parameters of the canonical Brusselator~\cite{epstein1998introduction,strogatz2024nonlinear} and $E$ and $M$ are the oscillating species, which are analogous to ERK and its regulator protein MEK~\cite{kholodenko2010signalling}. Because the level of ERK is strongly correlated with the cellular actin level~\cite{logue2015erk,de2022interplay}, in our model, the concentration of $E$ serves as a proxy for the cellular actin level. $D$ is a degrader of $E$. We have added the associated term based on the experimental observation that ERK is regularly degraded by some regulator proteins~\cite{moreno_competition_2019,shankaran_rapid_2009}. We assume that in the absence of any stimulus, its level is maintained at the homeostatic value $D_0$ with a characteristic recovery time $\tau_D$. The generic criterion for Hopf bifurcation in the presence of $D$ is complicated and not useful. However, some specific cases are of importance. Without $D$, this system undergoes a Hopf bifurcation to an oscillatory state when $b > 1 + a^2c$~\cite{epstein1998introduction}. Because ERK oscillation is widely observed in isolated cells, we choose $b = 3$, and $a = c = 1$ to ensure an oscillatory steady state. In the presence of $D$, when $\tau_D \rightarrow 0$, the Hopf bifurcation happens for $D_0 \lessapprox 0.11$ (Fig.~\ref{fig:0D}B). Here, we use $D_0 = 0.1$ for all our simulations to ensure that a single cell stays close to the bifurcation point~\cite{mora2011biological,munoz2018colloquium}. The corresponding limit cycle is shown in Fig.~\ref{fig:0D}B.  

\paragraph*{Mechanochemical coupling:} ERK activates cellular pathways that upregulates actomyosin level in the cell~\cite{logue2015erk,lavoie2020erk,hino2020erk,mendoza2011erk,mendoza2015erk,tanimura2017erk,barros2005activation}. Thus, increased ERK level (level of $E$ in the model) leads to cell contractions, which decreases the cell volume \cite{boocock_theory_2021,boocock_interplay_2023}. In our model, the decreased cell volume generates mechanical stress that upregulates $D$, which, in turn, downregulates $E$, leading to cellular expansion. This phenomenon can be captured by modifying Eqn.~\ref{Eq:Bruss1} as follows.
\begin{geqn}
\tau_D\del_t D = -(D-D_0) - \beta D(L-1)\\
\tau_L \del_t L_0 = -(L_0-1) - \alpha (E-E_0)\\
\tau_R \del_t L = -(L-L_0) + f(L,t)
\label{Eq:MCFL}
\end{geqn}
Here $L$ is the instantaneous length of the cell and $L_0$ is the homeostatic (rest) length of the cell, whereas $E_0$ is the $E$ concentration at the fixed point of Eqn.~\ref{Eq:Bruss1}. $\alpha$ determines how rapidly changes in $E$ modifies $L_0$ and $\beta$ determines how the instantaneous volume, $L$, changes the level of $D$. Together, $\mu = \alpha\beta$ determines the strength of the mechanochemical coupling \cite{boocock_theory_2021}. $f(L,t)$ is a forcing term that can arise due to external driving or interaction between cells in a network. For example, for a 1D continuum vertex model, the equations can be written as follows~\cite{boocock_theory_2021} (SI-III).   
\begin{geqn}
    \del_t M = a -(b+1)M + cM^2E \\
    \del_t E = bM - cM^2E - DE\\ 
    \tau_D\del_t \delta = -(\delta + \delta^3) - \beta D(\del_xR - 1)\\
    \tau_L \del_t l = -(l + l^3) - \alpha(E-E_0) \\
    %\alpha (\epsilon + \epsilon^3)\\
    \tau_R \del_t R = \del^2_xR - \del_xL_0
    \label{Eq:contvertex1D}
\end{geqn}
Here $R(x)$ is the location of the cell vertex and $\del_xR$ is the continuum equivalent of the cell length, $L$. $\tau_D$, $\tau_L$ are relaxation timescales, and $\tau_R = 1$ is the frictional timescale. Moreover, $\delta = D - D_0$ and $l = L_0 - 1 $. 
%The cubic terms involving these variables are included for numerical stability; they do not change the general behavior of the system. 
Physically, the model describes a spring network where a Brusselator governs each spring's mechanical state ($L_0$ and $L$). In the rest of the paper, we assume that there was no MCC prior to $t = 0$. Hence, we choose initial conditions from the uncoupled Brusselator limit cycle (Fig.~\ref{fig:0D}B). MCC is turned on at $t = 0$, resulting in changes in the stability of the uncoupled fixed point $\mvec{u_0} = \{M_0,E_0,D_0,1,1\}$, as the phase space becomes $5$-dimensional.      

\paragraph*{Compaction-driven oscillation death:} The MCFL-coupled single cell undergoes multiple bifurcations as $\mu$ is varied (Fig.~\ref{fig:0D}C). Below a $\tau_{D,L}$-independent threshold $\mu_c \approx 1.0566$, the cell displays canonical limit-cycle oscillations of Brusselators. At $\mu_c$, the determinant of the Jacobian matrix becomes zero (SI-II.B), and a fixed point suddenly appears, which splits into two saddle points as $\mu$ is increased (Fig.~\ref{fig:0D}C inset); a situation analogous to saddle-node bifurcation~\cite{strogatz2024nonlinear}. Closer inspection reveals that one of them favors compression ($L < 1$) and the other extension ($L \geq 1$, Fig~\ref{fig:0D}C-D). Furthermore, the fixed point corresponding to the compression branch becomes linearly stable for a range of $\mu$ values, which kills any oscillation in the system, a feature reminiscent of the COD of ERK in confluent tissues~\cite{shankaran_rapid_2009,moreno_competition_2019}. We find that this transition from an oscillatory to nonoscillatory state through compaction is a generic feature of the HBR and does not depend on $\tau_D$ and $\tau_L$ (Fig.S4). The model shows several bifurcations, such as at $\mu \approx 1.5$ in Fig~\ref{fig:0D}D. However, they are not generic features, and we will not explore them in detail here. 

%Here, we solely focus on COD and explore the collective behaviors of HBR in this regime.   


%The stability close to the bifurcation point depends on the $\tau_{D,L}$ values and the two fixed points can be both saddles, both nodes, or one saddle and one node (SI). However, 
%The stability of these two branches of fixed points The two fixed points describe different mechanical states of the single cell, with one branch favoring compression ($L < 1$) and the other stretching ($L>1$). Interestingly, the compression branch is linearly stable for a range of $\mu$ values. For these $\mu$ values, the Brusselator network stops oscillating and reaches a fixed point, a phenomena termed as oscillation death (OD). This is not the usual Hopf bifurcation, which happens in the middle branch at around $\mu = 1.1$, where the branch changes discontinuously. We find that the complex part of the maximum eigenvalue vanishes at this point (Fig.~\ref{fig:0D}D), potentially undergoing a subcritical Hopf bifurcation. A detailed bifurcation analysis of this model is beyond the scope of this paper. For now, it suffices to note that there exists a range of $\mu$ for which the system can switch from a limit cycle to a stable node (Fig.~\ref{fig:0D}C). 

