    \label{fig:spectra}
\end{figure}

\paragraph*{Non-Hermitian dynamics:} Next we consider the linear stability of Eqn.~\ref{Eq:contvertex1D} about $\mvec{u_0}$ (Fig.~\ref{fig:0D}A). Using solutions of the form $\Tilde{y}(\omega,q)\exp(\omega t + iqx)$ for a variable $y(t,x)$, we obtain an eigenvalue equation of the form $\omega \mathbf{\Tilde{u}} = \mat{A_q} \mathbf{\Tilde{u}}$, where, $\mathbf{\Tilde{u}} = [\Tilde{M},\Tilde{E},\Tilde{D},\Tilde{L_0},\Tilde{R}]^T$, and
\begin{equation}
\resizebox{0.9\columnwidth}{!}{$
    \mat{A_q} = \begin{bmatrix}
    -1 -b + c_1 & c_2 & 0 & 0 & 0\\
     b - c_1 & -(c_2 + D_0) & -E_0& 0& 0\\
     0 & 0 & -1/\tau_D & 0 & -i\beta D_0q/\tau_D\\
     0 & -\alpha/\tau_L & 0 & -1/\tau_L & 0\\
     0 & 0 & 0& -iq/\tau_R & -q^2/\tau_R
    \end{bmatrix}
    \label{Eq:Aq}
    $}
\end{equation}
Here, $c_1 = 2c M_0 E_0$ and $c_2 = c M_0^2$. Without any MCC (i.e. $\mu = \alpha\beta = 0$), $\mat{A_q}$ splits into two blocks, one describing the Brusselator chemistry and the other describing the spring network. The latter contains an exceptional point~\cite{ashida2020non} at $q = \sqrt{\tau_R/\tau_L}$ (SI-III.B). For $\mu > 0$, the coupling between the mechanical and the chemical components results in a line of broken $\mathcal{PT}$-symmetry that ends at two exceptional points at $q_{ep}^{\pm}$ when $\tau_D\neq\tau_L$, with $q_{ep}^{-}\neq0$ (nondegenerate, Fig.~\ref{fig:spectra}A). In contrast, when $\tau_D = \tau_L$, $q_{ep}^{-}$ merges with a diabolical point at $q = 0$ (Fig.~\ref{fig:spectra}B), which creates a Dirac cone like dispersion~\cite{ashida2020non}. The nondegenerate case also contains one diabolical point, but for Fig.~\ref{fig:spectra}A, it is in between $q_{ep}^{\pm}$. To understand the origin of the exceptional points, we calculated the eigenvalue spectrum using perturbation theory about the uncoupled state (SI-III.C, Fig.~\ref{fig:spectra}C). This calculation fails near $q_{ep}^{\pm}$, as expected~\cite{ashida2020non}. However, its failure shows that two pairs of eigenvectors collapse at the boundaries of the symmetry-broken region, implying that within this region, three eigenvectors collapse into one. Numerical analysis reveals the same. Moreover, the failure location of the perturbation theory also gives a near-accurate estimate of the boundaries of the symmetry-broken region (Fig.~\ref{fig:spectra}D). Specifically, it shows that the separation between $q_{ep}^{\pm}$ grows monotonically with $\mu$ (Fig.~\ref{fig:spectra}D inset). Numerical calculation of the spectra shows that indeed at sufficiently high $\mu$ (e.g. $\mu \geq 1 $, Fig.~\ref{fig:pattern}), $q_{ep}^- \rightarrow 0$, which implies that in this limit, collective behaviors arise due to broken $\mathcal{PT}$-symmetry, which can show spontaneous excitability and traveling waves~\cite{scheibner2020odd,banerjee2021active,dhanuka2025excitability}. 

