    \centering
    \includegraphics[width=\textwidth]{Fig3_3.pdf}
    \caption{\textbf{Non-hermitian pattern formation:} Kymograph of the length of springs, $\partial_xR$, and the spatial variation of $E$ at $t = 40000$ (black dashed line in the Kymograph) for (A) $\mu = 0.01 $, (B) $1$, and (C) $6.25$. In each case, $\alpha = \beta = \mu^{1/2}$. (D) The real (solid blue) part of the eigenvalues, $\omega (qa/\pi)$, does not have positive peaks at nonzero $q$ values, and hence, does not explain the instabilities that lead to the pattern formation. Similarly, the imaginary (dashed orange) part of $\omega(qa/\pi)$ does not vary linearly with $q$ and fails to explain the presence of traveling waves. This failure arises due to the nonorthogonality of the eigenvectors. Hence, (E) the growth rate of each component, $\Tilde{\omega}$ (see text), is needed to explain the observed patterns. For $\mu = 0.01$, the real part of $D$ and $E$ peaks near the exceptional point at $qa/\pi \approx 0.4$, explaining the observed pattern forming instability. The imaginary part of $D$ shows linear dependence on $q$, explaining the existence of localized traveling waves below the corresponding length scale. (F, G) Similar analysis shows that for $\mu = 1$, $L_0$ is the dominant mode and leads to the formation of regions with no oscillation (notice the log-scale on y-axis). (H, I) For $\mu = 6.25$, $L_0$ and $M$ are the dominant modes and lead to system-spanning traveling waves. System size = $200a$, Simulation time = $5\times10^4\tau_R$.}
