\end{figure*}

\paragraph*{Patterns:} The model generates a rich class of patterns (Fig.~\ref{fig:pattern}A-C) whose existence is not apparent from the eigenvalue spectra. For example, for $\alpha = \beta = 0.1$ (Fig.~\ref{fig:pattern}D), the modes with positive real eigenvalues are $q$-independent, implying no spatiotemporal patterns. Yet, from numerical solutions of Eqn.~\ref{Eq:contvertex1D}, we find nontrivial spatiotemporal patterns, such as localized traveling waves and chimera states (SI movie). This apparent failure of the linear stability analysis (LSA) arises due to the non-Hermiticity of $\mat{A_q}$, whose right eigenvectors, $\ket{\phi_n}$, are linearly independent, but not orthogonal to each other. This issue can be circumvented by writing each component of $\mathbf{\Tilde{u}}$ as a linear combination of $\ket{\phi_n}$, i.e., $\ket{\Tilde{u}_i} = \sum_n c_n^i \ket {\phi_n}$, and its biorthogonal conjugate $\bra{\Tilde{\Tilde{u}}_i}$ as a linear combination of the left eigenvectors  $\sum_m d_m^i\bra{\chi_m}$. From these two relations we get the eigenvalue of $\ket{\Tilde{u}_i}$ is: $\Tilde{\omega_i} = \sum_n d_n^i c_n^i \omega_n$, where $\omega_n$ is the eigenvalue of $\ket{\phi_n}$ (SI-III.D). Using these relations, we calculate the growth rate of each component for different coupling strengths $\mu = \alpha\beta$ (Fig.~\ref{fig:pattern}D-I). We find that for all of them, the exceptional points and the broken $\mathcal{PT}$-symmetry play a central role in determining the linear stability. For example, for $\alpha = \beta = 0.1$, the spatial modulation of $E$ arises from an unstable mode at $qa/\pi \approx 0.4$, near $q_{ep}^+$. (Fig.~\ref{fig:pattern}D-E). Similar behavior is seen for $\alpha = \beta = 2.5$ (Fig.~\ref{fig:pattern}H-I). However, the most unstable component changes from $D$ to $L_0$, shifting from chemistry-dominated to mechanics-dominated patterns. This transition becomes even more apparent for $\alpha = \beta = 1$, for which $M$, $D$, and $L_0$ have positive $\mbox{Re}(\Tilde{\omega})$. In fact, $\mbox{Re}(\Tilde{\omega})$ of $L_0$ is orders of magnitude larger than the other component, revealing its dominant role in the pattern formation. Inspection of the imaginary part of $\Tilde{\omega}$ shows linear variation with $q$ near $q_{ep}^+$ (Fig.~\ref{fig:pattern}E, I), revealing the presence of traveling waves in the system, as seen in the kymographs. Finally, for $\alpha = \beta = 1$, the instability in $L_0$ drives the formation of contiguous regions of COD, a situation consistent with the single cell model (Fig.~\ref{fig:0D}C), and similar to oscillation death observed in coupled nonlinear oscillators~\cite{koseska_oscillation_2013}. 

