\paragraph*{Dynamic phase transitions:} Patterns formed from mechanochemical instability lead to spatiotemporal variation in cell lengths. To understand this variation quantitatively, we computed the distribution of lengths, $\partial_xR$, from numerical simulations (Fig.~\ref{fig:distro}A) at $\tau_D = 1.1$ and $\tau_L = 1.2$ (for other $\tau_{D,L}$ values see Fig.S5-6). The distributions transition from unimodal to bimodal as $\mu$ is increased. This change can be quantified by finding the location of the two most prominent peaks in the distribution (Fig.~\ref{fig:distro}B). For $\mu \lessapprox 0.5$ (Region I), the distribution is mainly unimodal, although isolated points of bimodality are also observed. Length variation in this region predominantly results from oscillations about the rest length, $\hat{L}_0  = 1$, of the uncoupled HBR. Above this threshold (Region II), the distribution becomes bimodal, and the system bifurcates into two branches, one with $\partial_xR < 1$ (compression) and another with $\partial_xR > 1$ (extension), akin to Fig.~\ref{fig:0D}C. Indeed, we see clusters of COD in II. Total size of these clusters, $L_{OD}$, is comparable to the system size, $L_x$, for a range of parameters (Fig.~\ref{fig:distro}C). At $\mu \approx 3.24$ (Region III), the system undergoes another transition, and the length of a cell oscillate from $\partial_xR < 1$ to $\partial_xR > 1$ through intermittent traveling waves. Finally, lengths oscillate through traveling waves for $\mu > 5.60$ (Region IV). These transitions are similar to what we had observed for the single-cell model (Fig.~\ref{fig:0D}C) and resemble non-reciprocal phase transitions observed in many non-Hermitian systems~\cite{fruchart2021non}. Remarkably, at these bifurcation points, the state variables, such as $E$, show intermittent fluctuations (Fig.~\ref{fig:distro}D inset). The excess kurtosis, $\kappa$, of these fluctuations spikes to a high positive value near the boundaries and stays negative away from them (Fig.~\ref{fig:distro}D). A positive $\kappa$ implies a fat-tailed distribution, consistent with the intermittency in fluctuations~\cite{davidson2015turbulence} (SI-I.E). Indeed, we find that $\kappa$ diverges as a power law as the boundaries are approached (Fig.~\ref{fig:distro}E), signifying a dynamic phase transition (DPT) at each boundary~\cite{international_centre_for_theoretical_sciences_dynamical_2019,nyawo2017minimal}. The origin of intermittency is different at different DPTs. At $\mu = 0.5$, the available phase space expands and the limit cycle becomes diffused. At $\mu = 3.24$, A new limit cycle, corresponding to the mechanical traveling waves, appears inside the Brusselator limit cycle, leading to bistability in oscillation, akin to canard explosion~\cite{wechselberger2007canards}. Finally, at $\mu = 5.60$, the Brusselator limit cycle disappears and only the mechanical limit cycle remains (see SI movies). 

