\paragraph*{Phase diagram:} From these observations, we propose a phase diagram of the HBR (Fig.~\ref{fig:distro}F). It has four realizable phases, marked I-IV. In phase I ($\mu < 0.5$), chemistry dominates over mechanics, and the collective behavior of HBR is governed mainly by the Brusselator's chemistry. We observe chimera states~\cite{kuramoto2002coexistence,abrams2004chimera,martens_chimera_2013,das2024order}, and local \& ephemeral traveling waves in this region. In phase IV, mechanics dominates, and we observe system-spanning and stable traveling waves, akin to prior observations~\cite{boocock_theory_2021,boocock_interplay_2023}. In phases II and III, we observe features of both regions. Specifically, in III, we see bistable limit cycle oscillations. Finally, in II, we observe localized oscillations, ephemeral and local traveling pulses, and, most importantly, collective COD (SI movies). The traveling waves become evanescent at the boundaries of COD. The location of these phases varies with $\tau_D$ and $\tau_L$, but the qualitative features remain invariant.    

\paragraph*{Discussion:} 
In this paper, we investigated the origin of compaction-driven oscillation death (COD) by mechanochemical feedback (MCF). We have shown that the non-Hermitian dynamics of Hopf oscillators coupled through MCF spontaneously breaks $\mathcal{PT}$-symmetry, which gives rise to COD and many such nontrivial patterns. Although we have demonstrated our results only for a model system, HBR, the underlying symmetry of coupled Hopf oscillators~\cite{collins_coupled_1993,collins_group-theoretic_1994} ensures that our results and predictions hold generally for \textit{any} mechanochemically coupled Hopf oscillators. Indeed, we find remarkable agreement of our predictions with patterns observed in confluent epithelial tissues and in a 2D vertex model \footnote{Sindhu M. et al. unpublished data.}, which bolsters the generality of our predictions. Therefore, in addition to epithelial tissues~\cite{parmar2025spontaneous,boocock_theory_2021,ioratim-uba_mechanochemical_2023,banerjee2015propagating,perez-verdugo_excitable_2024,boocock_interplay_2023,moreno_competition_2019,shankaran_rapid_2009}, our predictions can be tested in a wide variety of experimental systems, such as in heart tissues~\cite{roshan2023multiscale}, microfluidic chemical droplets~\cite{tompkins2014testing,kumar2024emergent}, electromechanical Hopf oscillators~\cite{zhai2004amplitude}, active metamaterials~\cite{baconnier2022selective}, or synthetic cell mimics~\cite{arora2024shape}. 

