\subsection{Numerical solution of continuum model}
To simulate the coupled 1D Partial Differential Equations (PDE)~\ref{Eq:SIcontvertex1D}, we have implemented the central in-space method with Runge-Kutta time marching. We use spatial and temporal discretization with $a = \Delta x = 1$ and $\Delta t = 0.01$, satisfying the Courant–Friedrichs–Lewy (CFL) condition to ensure the stability of the numerical solutions. Here are the simulation details: 

\begin{enumerate}
    \item $a = 1$ and $\tau_R = 1$ for all simulations. They are the unit of length and time in our model. 

    \item For Fig 3A-C, we have simulated a model with $L_x = 200a$ and $t_{max} = 5\times10^4\tau_R$. A single seed value is shown.

    \item For Fig 4A-B, we have chosen $\alpha = \beta = \sqrt{\mu}$, where $\mu \in [1e-2,9]$. $L_x = 1000a$, $t_{max} = 10^5\tau_R$. The distributions were obtained from datapoints from  $t = 9.9\times10^4-10^5$ and averaged over $10$ random initial conditions. All together, $10^3\times10^3\times10 = 10^7$ samples were used to compute the distributions. 

    \item For Fig 4C and Fig.~\ref{fig:SI-total-size},~\ref{fig:SI-total-number}, we have chosen $\alpha,\beta = \{0.1,0.2,0.3,0.5,0.8,1,1.5,2,2.5,3\}$. For each of the 100 pairs of $\alpha,\beta$ values and 9 pairs of $\tau_D,\tau_L$ values, we used 20 random initial conditions. 

    \item For details of Fig. 4D, check section I.E. 

    \item For Fig.4E, we chose $\mu = \mu_c \pm \delta\mu$, where $\mu_c = 0.5, 3.24, \mbox{ and }5.60$ and $\delta\mu \in [10^{-6},10^{-1}]$. 10 different replicates were used. 



\end{enumerate}

