\subsection{$\tilde{\omega_i}$ from $\omega_n$}
The analysis is based on ref.~\cite{brody2013biorthogonal}. $\mat {A_q}$ is a non-Hermitian, with right eigenvectors $\ket{\phi_n}$ satisfying, $\mat{A_q}\ket{\phi_n} = \omega_n\ket{\phi_n}$. The left eigenvectors $\bra{\chi_m}$ satisfy $\bra{\chi_m}\mat{A_q} = \omega_m \bra{\chi_m}$. 

The $\ket{\phi_n}$ are linearly independent but not orthogonal to each other because of the non-Hermiticity of $\mat{A_q}$. Same holds for $\bra{\chi_m}$. However, they can be transformed in such a way that together they satisfy the biorthogonality condition 
\begin{equation}
    \bra{\chi_m}\ket{\phi_n} =\delta_{mn}.
\end{equation}

We can express the $\ket{\tilde{u}_i}$ in this basis through a linear transformation. To do so, note that:
\begin{geqn}
    \ket{\phi_n} = \sum_i p_i^n \ket{\tilde{u}_i}\\
    \bra{\chi_m} = \sum_i \bra{\tilde{\tilde{u}}_i}q_i^m
\end{geqn}
Here $\bra{\tilde{\tilde{u}}_i}$ is the left vector
corresponding to $\ket{\tilde{u}_i}$. We can express it in matrix form as: 
\begin{geqn}
    \ket{\mathbf{\phi}} = \mat{P}\ket{\mathbf{\tilde{u}}}\\
    \bra{\mathbf{\chi}} = \bra{\mathbf{\tilde{\tilde{u}}}}\mat{Q}
\end{geqn}
from which we can write: 
\begin{geqn}
    \mat{P^{-1}}\ket{\mathbf{\phi}} = \ket{\mathbf{\tilde{u}}}\\
    \bra{\mathbf{\chi}}\mat{Q^{-1}} = \bra{\mathbf{\tilde{\tilde{u}}}}.
\end{geqn}
From this, we can write each component as: 
\begin{geqn}
    \ket{\tilde{u}_i} = \sum_n c^i_n \ket{\phi_n}\\
    \bra{\tilde{\tilde{u}}_i} = \sum_i \bra{\chi_m} d^i_m.
    \label{eq:lin}
\end{geqn}
Here $c^i_n = (P^{-1})_{in}$ and $d^i_m = (Q^{-1})_{mi}$. Using the birothogonality relation $\braket{\chi_m}{\phi_n} = \delta_{mn}$, we get:
\begin{equation}
\braket{\tilde{\tilde{u}}_j}{\tilde{u}_i} = \sum_n d_n^jc_n^i .
\end{equation}
If $\{\ket{\tilde{u}_i}\}$ form a biorthogonal basis, as in our case, it also satisfies the relation: $\braket{\tilde{\tilde{u}}_j}{u_i} = \delta_{ij}$.

Now we use these relationships to obtain the eigenvalue, $\tilde{\omega}_i$ of $\ket{u_i}$. First, we apply $\mat{A_q}$ on Eqn.~\ref{eq:lin}. 
\begin{align}
    \mat A_q \ket{{\tilde{u}_i}} &= \sum_n c^i_n \mat A_q \ket{\phi_n}\\
    \therefore \tilde{\omega}_i \ket{{\tilde{u}_i}} &= \sum_n c^i_n\omega_n\ket{\phi_n} \label{eq:lin_eig}
\end{align}

Applying $\bra{\tilde{\tilde{u}}_i}$ on Eqn.~\ref{eq:lin_eig}, we get: 
\begin{align}
    \tilde{\omega}_i\bra{\tilde{\tilde{u}}_i}\ket{{\tilde{u}_i}} &= \sum_m\sum_n c^i_n d_m^i\omega_n\braket{\chi_m}{\phi_n}\\
    % \implies \braket{\tilde{u_i}}{u_i} &= \sum_n c^n_i d_n^i\omega_n\\
    \therefore \tilde{\omega}_i &= \sum_n c_n^i d_n^i\omega_n\label{eq:omega_tilde}
\end{align}

\subsection{Numerical calculation of $\tilde{\omega}$}
We use the SciPy linear algebra library (specifically, linalg.eig) to calculate the right and the left eigenvectors of $\mat{A_q}$. The algorithm introduces arbitrary phases in the calculated eigenvectors. While this is fine to get the eigenvalue spectra, $\omega(q)$, it introduces isolated, non-physical discontinuities in the $\tilde{\omega}$ calculation. 

To circumvent this issue and remove the unphysical discontinuities, we require that the inner-product between the left eigenvector at $q$ and the corresponding right eigenvector at $q+dq$ is continuous except near $q_{ep}^{\pm}$. This is akin to the $\mathbf{k}\cdot \mathbf{p}$-perturbation theory in solid-state physics and, hence, we refer to this procedure as $\mathbf{L}\cdot\mathbf{R}$-perturbation theory. Currently, we do not have an automated procedure to identify the isolated discontinuities and remove them. Therefore, we determine the $\tilde{\omega}(q)$ on a case-by-case basis. We have found that the corrections work for a sufficiently broad set of parameter values. For example, the corrections made for $\alpha = \beta = 1$ works for $\alpha = \beta \in [0.7,1.5]$ range, which is approximately, the size of region II in the phase diagram. In Fig.~\ref{fig:LR-pert}, we demonstrate this process.

