\begin{abstract}

	We study mean field portfolio games under Epstein-Zin preferences, which naturally encompass the classical time-additive power utility as a special case. 
 In a general non-Markovian framework, we establish a uniqueness result by proving a one-to-one correspondence between Nash equilibria and the solutions to a class of BSDEs.
	A key ingredient in our approach is a necessary stochastic maximum principle tailored to Epstein-Zin utility and a nonlinear transformation.
	In the deterministic setting, we further derive an explicit closed-form solution for the equilibrium investment and consumption policies.


%	We study mean field portfolio games with Epstein-Zin preferences, with the classical time additive
%power utility function included as a special case. Thus, this paper includes the first author's previous works \cite{Fu-2021,Fu2023} as specials cases. However, the setting with more general utility functions in this paper is by no means a purely technical extension of the first author's previous works. In a general non-Markovian framework, we establish a uniqueness result by establishing a one-to-one correspondence between the Nash equilibrium and the solution to some certain BSDE. To do so, a necessary stochastic maximum principle with Epstein-Zin preferences is required. Using this method, we do not need reverse H\"older's inequality as in \cite{Fu-2021,Fu2023}, thus, the regularity of the admissible equilibrium set, for which the uniqueness result holds, is significantly enlarged. In the deterministic setting, we also get a closed-form equilibrium investment strategy and consumption plan.
\end{abstract}

