We establish an existence of equilibrium result for such games with deterministic, though possibly time-varying model parameters, and a uniqueness of equilibrium result for general stochastic settings. Specifically, we prove that Nash equilibria in mean-field portfolio games with Epstein-Zin preferences are uniquely characterized in terms of a solution to a certain quadratic BSDE. Similar characterization results are often implicitly assumed - though rarely proved - in the literature.  For instance, \cite{Riedel-2024} proves the existence of a unique simple equilibrium assuming that optimizing the Epstein-Zin utility index is equivalent to optimizing the driver of some BSDE. This is not always the case, though; counterexample can easily be constructed. Our uniqueness result shows that the simple equilibrium obtained in \cite{Riedel-2024} is indeed the unique bounded equilibrium.  

