 \section{The mean field game with Epstein-Zin utility} \label{sec:EZ-utility}

In this section we recall the benchmark model of a single player optimization problem with Epstein-Zin preferences along with a game-theoretic extension with performance concerns. 

We fix a common time horizon $[0,T]$ for all investors and assume that all random variables are defined on a filtered probability space $(\Omega, \cF, (\cF_t)_{t \in [0,T]}, \bP)$ that carries independent Brownian motions $W^0, W^1,\cdots, W^N$ for some $N \in \bN$. The Brownian motions $W^1,\cdots, W^N$ capture {\sl idiosyncratic noises} in an $N$-player model while the Brownian motion $W^0$ captures a {\sl common noise} shared by all agents.  

 \subsection{Single agent benchmark model}

In a single agent benchmark model the agent can invest in a risk-free asset that pays interest at rates $(r_t)$ and in a risky asset whose price process $(S_t)$ follows the stochastic differential equation (SDE)
 \[
 	\frac{dS_t}{S_t} = R_t dt + \sigma_t dW^1_t + \sigma^0_t dW^0_t, \qquad t \in [0,T].
 \] 

 \begin{assumption}
	The processes $(r_t)$, $(R_t)$, $(\sigma_t)$ and $(\sigma^0_t)$ are progressively measurable and bounded, and $(\sigma)^2+(\sigma^0)^2$ is strictly positive.
 \end{assumption}
We denote by $\Pi=(\Pi_t)$ and $C=(C_t)$ a pair of progressively measurable processes that represent the investor’s investment strategy and consumption plan, respectively. Specifically, $\Pi_t$ denotes the amount of money invested in the risky stock and $C_t$ denotes the amount of money consumed at time $t \in [0,T]$. The corresponding wealth process $X^{\Pi, C}$ then evolves according to the SDE
\[
	dX^{\Pi, C}_t = r_t X^{\Pi, C}_t\, dt + \Pi_t h_t\, dt + \Pi_t \sigma_t\, dW_t + \pi_t \sigma^0_t\, dW^0_t - C_t\, dt, \qquad t \in [0,T],
\]
where $h:=R-r$ is the risk premium. 
%\begin{itemize}
%	\item $r_t$ is the {risk-free interest rate} (i.e., the return on the riskless asset),
%	\item $h_t := R_t - r_t$ is the {risk premium},
%	\item $R_t$ is the expected return of the risky asset,
%	\item $\sigma_t$ is the {idiosyncratic volatility} of the risky asset,
%	\item $\sigma^0_t$ represents the {systematic (common) volatility},
%	\item $(W_t, W^0_t)$ form a {two-dimensional Brownian motion} defined on a filtered probability space $(\Omega, \mathcal{F}, \mathbb{P})$, where:
%	\begin{itemize}
%		\item $W_t$ captures {idiosyncratic noise} (specific to the individual),
%		\item $W^0_t$ captures {common noise} (shared across agents).
%	\end{itemize}
%\end{itemize}
In a model without mean-field interaction the dynamics of the investor's Epstein-Zin utility from consumption is given by  
 \begin{equation}\label{EZ-0}
 	\begin{split}
 		\tilde V^{C}_t=&~\mathbb E\left[ \left.  \int_t^T  \tilde f(C_s,\tilde V^{C}_s)\,ds + \alpha U(C_T) \right|\mathcal F_t     \right], \qquad t \in [0,T],
 	%	=&~~\mathbb E\left[ \left.  \int_0^T  f(c_s,V^{\pi,c}_s)\,ds + U(X^{\pi,c}_T) \right|\mathcal F_t     \right]		- \int_0^t f(c_s,V^{\pi,c}_s)\,ds,					
 	\end{split}
 \end{equation}
where the {\sl aggregator function} $f:[0,\infty) \times (-\infty,0] \to \bR$ is given by
% \[
% 	\tilde f(c,v) := \delta \frac{(1-\gamma)v}{1-\psi^{-1}}\left( \left( \frac{c}{((1-\gamma)v)^{\frac{1}{1-\psi}}}\right)^{1-\frac{1}{\Psi}} -1\right).
% \] 
\begin{equation}\label{aggregator}
	f(c,v) = \frac{	 \delta c^{1-\frac{1}{\psi}}		}{1-\frac{1}{\psi}} \{  (1-\gamma)v     \}^{1-\frac{1}{\widetilde\theta}}  - \delta\widetilde\theta v,  \quad \widetilde{\theta} := \frac{1 - \gamma}{1 - \frac{1}{\psi}}.
\end{equation}
Here $\delta>0$ represent the {discount rate}, $\gamma >0$ specifies the {relative risk aversion}, $\psi >0$ represents the {elasticity of intertemporal substitution} (EIS), $\alpha > 0$ is a {rate of bequest}, and  the {\sl bequest function} $U$ is of power type, i.e.
\begin{equation}\label{power}
	U(x)=\frac{x^{1-\gamma}}{1-\gamma}. 
\end{equation}
\begin{assumption}
We assume throughout this paper that\footnote{Technically, our results also hold under the conditions $\psi \gamma \leq 1$ and $\gamma \leq 1$. However, the empirically relevant regime is $\psi > 1$ and $\gamma > 1$; see \cite{Xing2017}. The choice in \eqref{parameters} allows for a unified treatment of Epstein-Zin and power utility.}
\begin{equation}\label{parameters}
	{\psi \gamma \geq 1, \quad \psi \geq 1. }
\end{equation}
\end{assumption}
It is assumed that all wealth is consumed at the terminal time, i.e. $C_T=X^{\Pi,C}_T$. For this reason, the utility is also denoted by $\tilde V^{\Pi,C}$ to emphasize the dependence on both investment and consumption strategies.


